{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LWsPEgYg8AimzTjvdnH7oM","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t% The pathwidth complex -- first full draft (Ralph loop0008 item 08, 2026-10-03).\n2\t%\n3\t% Build: `make` in this directory (latexmk -pdf). Tables in tables/ are\n4\t% generated by `python -m paper2.latex.make_tables`; the bibliography is\n5\t% checked by `python -m paper2.latex.check_refs`. See README.md here.\n6\t%\n7\t% Class: `article`. The INFORMS Journal on Computing class was not obtained:\n8\t% INFORMS's author pages refused scripted access on 2026-10-03 (HTTP 403), so\n9\t% the template is \"to obtain\" and the formatting below (one column, 11 pt,\n10\t% author-year citations) is a stand-in, not the journal's stated rules.\n11\t\\documentclass[11pt,letterpaper]{article}\n12\t\n13\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n14\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n15\t\\usepackage{mathptmx}\n16\t\\usepackage{courier}\n17\t\\usepackage[protrusion=true,expansion=false]{microtype}\n18\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n19\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,amsthm}\n20\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n21\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n22\t\\usepackage{array}\n23\t\\usepackage{colortbl}\n24\t\\usepackage{tabularx}\n25\t\\usepackage{longtable}\n26\t\\usepackage{caption}\n27\t\\usepackage{float}\n28\t\\floatstyle{ruled}\n29\t\\newfloat{algorithm}{tbp}{loa}[section]\n30\t\\floatname{algorithm}{Algorithm}\n31\t\\usepackage[section]{placeins}\n32\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n33\t\\usepackage[hyphens]{url}\n34\t\\usepackage[authoryear,round]{natbib}\n35\t\\usepackage[colorlinks=true,linkcolor=blue!50!black,citecolor=blue!50!black,urlcolor=blue!50!black]{hyperref}\n36\t\n37\t\\graphicspath{{../figures/}}\n38\t\\numberwithin{figure}{section}\n39\t\\numberwithin{table}{section}\n40\t% Yanasse's Pesquisa Operacional paper prints as 1997a and his EJOR paper as\n41\t% 1997b, as in the project's documents: the EJOR key is Yanasse1997b (refs.bib).\n42\t\\captionsetup{font=small,labelfont=bf}\n43\t\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n44\t\n45\t% Lean names live in Appendix A (sec9a_appendix_lean.tex), one entry per\n46\t% result. In the text, \\leanref{key} prints \"(Appendix A, A.n)\". In the\n47\t% appendix, \\leanentry{key}{result} opens a row and \\leannames{a, b} prints the\n48\t% names with breaks at the underscores and dots; check_lean.py reads them.\n49\t\\makeatletter\n50\t\\g@addto@macro{\\UrlBreaks}{\\do\\_\\do\\.}\n51\t\\newcommand{\\leannames}[1]{\\def\\lean@sep{}\\@for\\lean@n:=#1\\do{\\lean@sep\\expandafter\\path\\expandafter{\\lean@n}\\def\\lean@sep{, }}}\n52\t\\makeatother\n53\t\\newcounter{leanentry}\n54\t\\renewcommand{\\theleanentry}{A.\\arabic{leanentry}}\n55\t\\newcommand{\\leanentry}[2]{\\leavevmode\\refstepcounter{leanentry}\\label{lean:#1}\\theleanentry & #2 &}\n56\t\\newcommand{\\leangroup}[1]{\\multicolumn{3}{@{}l}{\\emph{#1}}\\\\}\n57\t\\newcommand{\\leanref}[1]{(Appendix~\\ref{app:lean}, \\ref{lean:#1})}\n58\t\n59\t\\theoremstyle{plain}\n60\t\\newtheorem{theorem}{Theorem}[section]\n61\t\\newtheorem{lemma}[theorem]{Lemma}\n62\t\\newtheorem{proposition}[theorem]{Proposition}\n63\t\\newtheorem{corollary}[theorem]{Corollary}\n64\t\\theoremstyle{definition}\n65\t\\newtheorem{definition}[theorem]{Definition}\n66\t\\newtheorem{counterexample}[theorem]{Counterexample}\n67\t\\theoremstyle{remark}\n68\t\\newtheorem*{remark}{Remark}\n69\t\n70\t\\newcommand{\\pw}{\\mathrm{pw}}\n71\t\\newcommand{\\vs}{\\mathrm{vs}}\n72\t\\newcommand{\\ns}{\\mathrm{ns}}\n73\t\\newcommand{\\mns}{\\mathrm{mns}}\n74\t\\newcommand{\\es}{\\mathrm{es}}\n75\t\\newcommand{\\pes}{\\mathrm{pes}}\n76\t\\newcommand{\\sbw}{\\mathrm{sb}}\n77\t\\newcommand{\\cw}{\\mathrm{cw}}\n78\t\\newcommand{\\mcw}{\\mathrm{mcw}}\n79\t\\newcommand{\\cl}{\\mathrm{cl}}\n80\t\\newcommand{\\Sol}{\\mathrm{Sol}}\n81\t\\newcommand{\\cost}{\\mathrm{cost}}\n82\t\\newcommand{\\open}{\\mathrm{open}}\n83\t\\newcommand{\\close}{\\mathrm{close}}\n84\t\\newcommand{\\fin}{\\mathrm{fin}}\n85\t\\newcommand{\\pla}{\\mathrm{pla}}\n86\t\\newcommand{\\VSG}{\\mathrm{VSG}}\n87\t\\newcommand{\\placeholder}[1]{\\par\\noindent\\fbox{\\parbox{\\dimexpr\\linewidth-2\\fboxsep-2\\fboxrule}{\\textbf{Placeholder.} #1}}\\par}\n88\t\n89\t\\title{The pathwidth complex}\n90\t% Affiliations are not invented: the owner fills them in.\n91\t\\author{Alexandre Linhares\\\\{\\small\\fbox{affiliation to be added}} \\and\n92\tHoracio Hideki Yanasse\\\\{\\small\\fbox{affiliation to be added}}}\n93\t\\date{Draft, 3 October 2026}\n94\t\n95\t\\begin{document}\n96\t\\maketitle\n97\t\n98\t\\begin{abstract}\n99\t\\noindent\n100\tThe minimization of open stacks problem (MOSP) asks for the order in which to\n101\tmake a set of patterns so that the fewest customer orders are open at once.\n102\t\\citet{LY2002} listed twelve problems from operations research, VLSI design and\n103\tgraph theory, MOSP among them, as equal to it ``plus or minus one'', and gave\n104\tno proofs. We ask which of these equalities are true, and what follows when\n105\tresults, algorithms and benchmarks are moved between the twelve problems\n106\tthrough pathwidth, the quantity that connects them. We find four things. First,\n107\teight of the twelve rows are exact, counting pathwidth itself: each equals\n108\tpathwidth or pathwidth plus one. Two lie in bands one and two wide, and two are\n109\tfalse as stated; every relation is proved, and machine-checked in Lean.\n110\tSecond, the literature has settled on the name pathwidth, used more than three\n111\ttimes as often as the other eleven names together, and the three communities\n112\trarely cite one another. Third, the best exact algorithm for MOSP, the search of\n113\t\\citet{ChuStuckey2009}, is a pathwidth algorithm, and two of its published\n114\tdominance theorems are false. The correct rule was already known for\n115\tpathwidth; we show that it is tested by one bipartite matching per candidate,\n116\tand prove the repaired search sound. In our implementation it costs at most\n117\t2\\% more search nodes on each benchmark set, and it re-proved all 115 benchmark\n118\toptima that rested on the published rules, changing none. Fourth, we release\n119\t17,714 benchmark graphs, distinct up to isomorphism, from twenty collections,\n120\t16,087 of them with a certified pathwidth and a layout that attains it.\n121\t\\end{abstract}\n122\t\n123\t\\input{sec1_intro}\n124\t\\input{sec2_equivalences}\n125\t\\input{sec3_names}\n126\t\\input{sec4_search}\n127\t\\input{sec5_results}\n128\t\\input{sec6_conclusion}\n129\t\\input{sec7_data}\n130\t\n131\t\\bibliography{refs}\n132\t\n133\t\\appendix\n134\t\\input{sec9a_appendix_lean}\n135\t\\input{sec9b_appendix_machinery}\n136\t\\input{sec9c_appendix_names}\n137\t\n138\t\\end{document}\n139\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b19c00ce-b033-4bb1-a352-ad2cf0b0fbf8","uuid":"5bd3452c-d3ab-48a9-b5ad-f220c2a7fb89","timestamp":"2026-10-06T18:43:37.503Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/al/dev/MOSP/paper2/latex/main.tex","content":"% The pathwidth complex -- first full draft (Ralph loop0008 item 08, 2026-10-03).\n%\n% Build: `make` in this directory (latexmk -pdf). Tables in tables/ are\n% generated by `python -m paper2.latex.make_tables`; the bibliography is\n% checked by `python -m paper2.latex.check_refs`. See README.md here.\n%\n% Class: `article`. The INFORMS Journal on Computing class was not obtained:\n% INFORMS's author pages refused scripted access on 2026-10-03 (HTTP 403), so\n% the template is \"to obtain\" and the formatting below (one column, 11 pt,\n% author-year citations) is a stand-in, not the journal's stated rules.\n\\documentclass[11pt,letterpaper]{article}\n\n\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage{mathptmx}\n\\usepackage{courier}\n\\usepackage[protrusion=true,expansion=false]{microtype}\n\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,amsthm}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{array}\n\\usepackage{colortbl}\n\\usepackage{tabularx}\n\\usepackage{longtable}\n\\usepackage{caption}\n\\usepackage{float}\n\\floatstyle{ruled}\n\\newfloat{algorithm}{tbp}{loa}[section]\n\\floatname{algorithm}{Algorithm}\n\\usepackage[section]{placeins}\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage[hyphens]{url}\n\\usepackage[authoryear,round]{natbib}\n\\usepackage[colorlinks=true,linkcolor=blue!50!black,citecolor=blue!50!black,urlcolor=blue!50!black]{hyperref}\n\n\\graphicspath{{../figures/}}\n\\numberwithin{figure}{section}\n\\numberwithin{table}{section}\n% Yanasse's Pesquisa Operacional paper prints as 1997a and his EJOR paper as\n% 1997b, as in the project's documents: the EJOR key is Yanasse1997b (refs.bib).\n\\captionsetup{font=small,labelfont=bf}\n\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n\n% Lean names live in Appendix A (sec9a_appendix_lean.tex), one entry per\n% result. In the text, \\leanref{key} prints \"(Appendix A, A.n)\". In the\n% appendix, \\leanentry{key}{result} opens a row and \\leannames{a, b} prints the\n% names with breaks at the underscores and dots; check_lean.py reads them.\n\\makeatletter\n\\g@addto@macro{\\UrlBreaks}{\\do\\_\\do\\.}\n\\newcommand{\\leannames}[1]{\\def\\lean@sep{}\\@for\\lean@n:=#1\\do{\\lean@sep\\expandafter\\path\\expandafter{\\lean@n}\\def\\lean@sep{, }}}\n\\makeatother\n\\newcounter{leanentry}\n\\renewcommand{\\theleanentry}{A.\\arabic{leanentry}}\n\\newcommand{\\leanentry}[2]{\\leavevmode\\refstepcounter{leanentry}\\label{lean:#1}\\theleanentry & #2 &}\n\\newcommand{\\leangroup}[1]{\\multicolumn{3}{@{}l}{\\emph{#1}}\\\\}\n\\newcommand{\\leanref}[1]{(Appendix~\\ref{app:lean}, \\ref{lean:#1})}\n\n\\theoremstyle{plain}\n\\newtheorem{theorem}{Theorem}[section]\n\\newtheorem{lemma}[theorem]{Lemma}\n\\newtheorem{proposition}[theorem]{Proposition}\n\\newtheorem{corollary}[theorem]{Corollary}\n\\theoremstyle{definition}\n\\newtheorem{definition}[theorem]{Definition}\n\\newtheorem{counterexample}[theorem]{Counterexample}\n\\theoremstyle{remark}\n\\newtheorem*{remark}{Remark}\n\n\\newcommand{\\pw}{\\mathrm{pw}}\n\\newcommand{\\vs}{\\mathrm{vs}}\n\\newcommand{\\ns}{\\mathrm{ns}}\n\\newcommand{\\mns}{\\mathrm{mns}}\n\\newcommand{\\es}{\\mathrm{es}}\n\\newcommand{\\pes}{\\mathrm{pes}}\n\\newcommand{\\sbw}{\\mathrm{sb}}\n\\newcommand{\\cw}{\\mathrm{cw}}\n\\newcommand{\\mcw}{\\mathrm{mcw}}\n\\newcommand{\\cl}{\\mathrm{cl}}\n\\newcommand{\\Sol}{\\mathrm{Sol}}\n\\newcommand{\\cost}{\\mathrm{cost}}\n\\newcommand{\\open}{\\mathrm{open}}\n\\newcommand{\\close}{\\mathrm{close}}\n\\newcommand{\\fin}{\\mathrm{fin}}\n\\newcommand{\\pla}{\\mathrm{pla}}\n\\newcommand{\\VSG}{\\mathrm{VSG}}\n\\newcommand{\\placeholder}[1]{\\par\\noindent\\fbox{\\parbox{\\dimexpr\\linewidth-2\\fboxsep-2\\fboxrule}{\\textbf{Placeholder.} #1}}\\par}\n\n\\title{The pathwidth complex}\n% Affiliations are not invented: the owner fills them in.\n\\author{Alexandre Linhares\\\\{\\small\\fbox{affiliation to be added}} \\and\nHoracio Hideki Yanasse\\\\{\\small\\fbox{affiliation to be added}}}\n\\date{Draft, 3 October 2026}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\n\\noindent\nThe minimization of open stacks problem (MOSP) asks for the order in which to\nmake a set of patterns so that the fewest customer orders are open at once.\n\\citet{LY2002} listed twelve problems from operations research, VLSI design and\ngraph theory, MOSP among them, as equal to it ``plus or minus one'', and gave\nno proofs. We ask which of these equalities are true, and what follows when\nresults, algorithms and benchmarks are moved between the twelve problems\nthrough pathwidth, the quantity that connects them. We find four things. First,\neight of the twelve rows are exact, counting pathwidth itself: each equals\npathwidth or pathwidth plus one. Two lie in bands one and two wide, and two are\nfalse as stated; every relation is proved, and machine-checked in Lean.\nSecond, the literature has settled on the name pathwidth, used more than three\ntimes as often as the other eleven names together, and the three communities\nrarely cite one another. Third, the best exact algorithm for MOSP, the search of\n\\citet{ChuStuckey2009}, is a pathwidth algorithm, and two of its published\ndominance theorems are false. The correct rule was already known for\npathwidth; we show that it is tested by one bipartite matching per candidate,\nand prove the repaired search sound. In our implementation it costs at most\n2\\% more search nodes on each benchmark set, and it re-proved all 115 benchmark\noptima that rested on the published rules, changing none. Fourth, we release\n17,714 benchmark graphs, distinct up to isomorphism, from twenty collections,\n16,087 of them with a certified pathwidth and a layout that attains it.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\input{sec1_intro}\n\\input{sec2_equivalences}\n\\input{sec3_names}\n\\input{sec4_search}\n\\input{sec5_results}\n\\input{sec6_conclusion}\n\\input{sec7_data}\n\n\\bibliography{refs}\n\n\\appendix\n\\input{sec9a_appendix_lean}\n\\input{sec9b_appendix_machinery}\n\\input{sec9c_appendix_names}\n\n\\end{document}\n","numLines":139,"startLine":1,"totalLines":139}}}
