{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Rdfr2n71BQu6fXiVnxm9aw","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t% Section 4. Sources: paper2/revised_algorithm.md (4.1-4.6), search_soundness.md,\n2\t% prior_art_counterexample.md (prior art). Lean: lean/MOSPFormalization/Search/,\n3\t% names in Appendix A (sec9a_appendix_lean.tex). The old 4.6-4.7 are Section 5.\n4\t% Restructured 2026-10-06 (loop0009 item 05, review changes 4 and 5): stakes\n5\t% first, the pathwidth reading and the dictionary in 4.1, Algorithm 4.1 in 4.2,\n6\t% each rule as intuition -> counterexample -> repair -> test.\n7\t% Page numbers for Chu & Stuckey (2009) are those of the preprint\n8\t% literature/chu_stuckey_2009.pdf; for Chu (2011) the printed ones.\n9\t\\section{An exact open-stacks search, read as a pathwidth algorithm}\n10\t\\label{sec:search}\n11\t\n12\tIf MOSP is pathwidth (Theorem~\\ref{thm:mosp}), then the best exact MOSP\n13\talgorithm is a pathwidth algorithm, and its rules can be checked against what\n14\tthe pathwidth literature knows. This section does that for the \\emph{customer\n15\tsearch} of \\citet{ChuStuckey2009}, the search that Section~\\ref{sec:names}\n16\ttraced from the open-stacks challenge. It searches over the orders in which the\n17\tcustomers' stacks close, not over pattern sequences, and it prunes with\n18\tdominance rules. Three of the rules are stated as theorems (their Theorems~1,\n19\t2 and~3) and a fourth, the subset rule, in their \\S2. \\citet{ChuStuckey2009}\n20\tplace MOSP among ``graph path-width and gate matrix layout'', citing\n21\t\\citet{LY2002}, but they do not use pathwidth in the search. Any graph is the\n22\tMOSP graph of the instance with one customer per vertex and one pattern per\n23\tedge, so the search decides pathwidth for every graph.\n24\t\n25\tThe stakes are these. Two of the published dominance theorems, the\n26\t\\emph{definite move} and the \\emph{better move}, are false, and so are their\n27\trestatements in Chu's thesis \\citep{Chu2011} and, with a stronger premise, in\n28\t\\citet{Fink2012}. Yet no wrong answer is known: no whole instance on which the\n29\tpublished rules answer ``no'' wrongly has been found, and when every benchmark\n30\toptimum that rested on the search was proved again with repaired rules, no\n31\tvalue changed (Section~\\ref{sec:results}). The correct form of the definite\n32\tmove was already known for pathwidth, as Tamaki's commitment lemma, and we\n33\tshow that it can be tested by one small bipartite matching per candidate. The\n34\tmain result, Theorem~\\ref{thm:main}, says informally: \\emph{with both rules\n35\trepaired, the search is sound; whenever it answers that $k$ stacks do not\n36\tsuffice, they do not, and the graph has pathwidth at least $k$.} The repair\n37\tcosts at most 2\\% more search nodes on each benchmark set\n38\t(Section~\\ref{sec:results}).\n39\t\n40\tSection~\\ref{sec:search-def} defines the search and shows that it decides\n41\tpathwidth, in both vocabularies. Section~\\ref{sec:algorithm} gives the\n42\talgorithm. Sections~\\ref{sec:definite} and~\\ref{sec:other-rules} take the\n43\trules one at a time, Section~\\ref{sec:theorem} proves the repaired search\n44\tsound, and Section~\\ref{sec:layouts} places each rule in the pathwidth\n45\tliterature.\n46\t\n47\t\\subsection{The search decides pathwidth}\n48\t\\label{sec:search-def}\n49\t\n50\t\\citet[p.~4]{ChuStuckey2009} observe that the patterns matter only through the\n51\tMOSP graph. So fix a graph $G=(C,E)$ whose vertices are the customers, and an\n52\tinteger $k\\ge0$. In this section $k$ bounds the number of open stacks, which is\n53\tone more than a width: $k$ stacks suffice exactly when the pathwidth is at most\n54\t$k-1$ (Theorem~\\ref{thm:decides}). For a customer $c$ write $N[c]$ for its\n55\tclosed neighbourhood, the stacks that must be open when $c$'s stack closes. For\n56\ta set $T\\subseteq C$ of vertices, its \\emph{border} $\\partial T$ is the set of\n57\tvertices outside $T$ with a neighbour in $T$. For a set $T$ of closed\n58\tcustomers write\n59\t\\begin{align*}\n60\tO(T) &= \\textstyle\\bigcup_{c\\in T}N[c] && \\text{the stacks opened once $T$ has closed},\\\\\n61\t\\fin(X) &= \\{c : N[c]\\subseteq X\\} && \\text{the customers free to close when $X$ is open},\\\\\n62\t\\cl(T) &= \\fin(O(T)) && \\text{the \\emph{free closure} of $T$},\\\\\n63\tb(T) &= |O(T)\\setminus T| = |\\partial T| && \\text{the stacks open after $T$ has closed},\\\\\n64\to(c,T) &= N[c]\\setminus O(T),\\quad \\open(c,T)=|o(c,T)| && \\text{the stacks closing $c$ would newly open.}\n65\t\\end{align*}\n66\tThe open stacks after $T$ has closed are exactly the border of $T$, and this\n67\tone identity carries the whole translation to pathwidth.\n68\t\n69\tA \\emph{move} at $T$ closes one customer $c\\notin T$, at cost\n70\t$\\cost(T,c)=|O(T\\cup\\{c\\})\\setminus T| = b(T\\cup\\{c\\})+1$, the stacks open at\n71\tthat moment counting $c$. It is \\emph{playable} when $\\cost(T,c)\\le k$. Write\n72\t$P_k(T)$ when some order of $C\\setminus T$ has every step playable. On entering\n73\ta node the search closes every free customer, so it stands only at\n74\t\\emph{states}, sets with $S=\\cl(S)$. The \\emph{child} of $S$ by $c$ is\n75\t$S\\cdot c=\\cl(S\\cup\\{c\\})$. The search's predicate $\\Sol_k(S)$ holds when\n76\t$S=C$, or when some playable $c$ has $\\Sol_k(S\\cdot c)$~\\leanref{defs}. Every\n77\tnode the search visits satisfies the invariant $b(S)\\le k$.\n78\t\n79\t\\paragraph{An example.} Take the graph of Figure~\\ref{fig:example}(d), with\n80\t$k=3$. At $T=\\{a\\}$ we have $O(T)=\\{a,b\\}$, so one stack, $b$, is open:\n81\t$b(T)=1$ and $\\partial T=\\{b\\}$. No other customer is free, since $N[b]$\n82\tcontains $c$ and $d$, so $T$ is a state. Closing $b$ newly opens\n83\t$o(b,T)=\\{c,d\\}$ and costs $\\cost(T,b)=3$, the stacks $b$, $c$ and $d$; the\n84\tmove is playable. At $\\{a,b,c\\}$ every neighbour of $d$ is open, so $d$ is\n85\tfree, and the child $\\{a,b\\}\\cdot c$ is $\\{a,b,c,d\\}$. The closing order\n86\t$a,b,c,e$ (with $d$ and $f$ closed free) costs $2,3,3,2$, the profile of\n87\torder~A in Figure~\\ref{fig:example}(a).\n88\t\n89\t\\begin{lemma}[free moves never hurt]\\label{lem:F}\n90\tIf $b(T)\\le k$, then $P_k(T)\\iff\\Sol_k(\\cl(T))$~\\leanref{F}.\n91\t\\end{lemma}\n92\tClosing a customer whose neighbourhood is already open opens nothing, never\n93\tmakes a later step dearer, and leaves one fewer stack open. The hypothesis is\n94\tneeded: closing the free customers one by one costs $b(T)$ at each step.\n95\t\n96\t\\begin{theorem}[the search decides pathwidth]\\label{thm:decides}\n97\tFor nonempty $C$, $\\Sol_k(\\emptyset)\\iff\\pw(G)+1\\le k$. On the MOSP graph of an\n98\tinstance $M$ with at least one 1, $\\Sol_k(\\emptyset)\\iff Z(M)\\le k$~\\leanref{decides}.\n99\t\\end{theorem}\n100\t\\begin{proof}\n101\tFor a closing order $c_1,\\dots,c_n$ with prefixes $S_i$, the $i$-th step costs\n102\t$1+|\\partial S_i|$. Reverse the order to get a layout. Its first $n-i$\n103\tvertices are $C\\setminus S_i$, and those with a neighbour among the remaining\n104\tones are exactly $\\partial S_i$. So the cost of the closing order is the vertex\n105\tseparation of the reversed layout plus one, and Lemma~\\ref{lem:F} at the root\n106\tand Theorem~\\ref{thm:vs} finish the proof.\n107\t\\end{proof}\n108\t\n109\tSo a refutation, $\\neg\\Sol_k(\\emptyset)$, may only mean $\\pw(G)>k-1$. The rest\n110\tof the section asks whether the pruning rules preserve that meaning.\n111\t\n112\t\\paragraph{In pathwidth language.} The exact pathwidth literature writes\n113\t$N(T)$ for the border and $d(T)$ for its size\n114\t\\citep[p.~390]{KobayashiKomuroTamaki2014}; we keep $\\partial T$ and $b(T)$. A\n115\tvertex sequence is \\emph{$w$-feasible} when every prefix has border at most\n116\t$w$, and $\\vs(G)$ is the least $w$ for which some permutation is $w$-feasible.\n117\tTable~\\ref{tab:dictionary} translates each notion of the search into this\n118\tvocabulary. Read it row by row: every ingredient of the customer search is a\n119\tknown ingredient of an exact pathwidth algorithm, and the last two rows are\n120\tthe rules of Section~\\ref{sec:algorithm}.\n121\t\n122\t\\begin{table}[t]\n123\t\\centering\\small\n124\t\\caption{The customer search is, ingredient by ingredient, an exact pathwidth\n125\talgorithm: each notion of the search beside its name in the pathwidth\n126\tliterature. The memo and the definite move are described in\n127\tSection~\\ref{sec:algorithm}.}\n128\t\\label{tab:dictionary}\n129\t\\begin{tabularx}{\\linewidth}{@{}>{\\raggedright}p{5.2cm}>{\\raggedright\\arraybackslash}X@{}}\n130\t\\toprule\n131\tCustomer search & Layout \\\\\n132\t\\midrule\n133\tclosing order from $\\emptyset$ & vertex sequence, read forwards \\\\\n134\topen stacks $b(T)$ & border size $|\\partial T|$ \\\\\n135\t$\\cost(T,c)$ & $|\\partial(T\\cup\\{c\\})|+1$ \\\\\n136\t$\\Sol_k(\\emptyset)$ & $\\vs(G)\\le k-1$ \\\\\n137\tfree closure $\\cl(T)$ & full set of $T$ \\citep[p.~328]{SuchanVillanger2009} \\\\\n138\tmemo & failure table \\\\\n139\tdefinite move, repaired & commitment to the child \\\\\n140\t\\bottomrule\\end{tabularx}\n141\t\\end{table}\n142\t\n143\t\\subsection{The algorithm}\n144\t\\label{sec:algorithm}\n145\t\n146\tAlgorithm~\\ref{alg:search} gives the customer search. It is a depth-first\n147\tsearch over states. At each node it closes the free customers, consults a\n148\ttable of states already refuted, keeps the playable candidates, filters them\n149\twith the dominance rules and recurses on the survivors.\n150\t\n151\t\\begin{algorithm}[t]\n152\t\\caption{The customer search \\citep{ChuStuckey2009} is a plain depth-first\n153\tsearch; all its pruning sits in the memo (lines~4 and~12), the filter\n154\t(line~6) and the old moves (line~9). It decides $\\Sol_k(S)$ for a graph\n155\t$G=(C,E)$ and a stack bound $k$, and is called as\n156\t$\\textsc{Search}(\\emptyset,\\emptyset)$. $Q$ is the set of old moves the node\n157\tinherits.}\n158\t\\label{alg:search}\n159\t\\small\n160\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}r@{\\quad}l@{\\qquad}l@{}}\n161\t1 & $\\textsc{Search}(S,Q)$: & \\\\\n162\t2 & \\quad $S\\gets\\cl(S)$ & \\textcolor{gray}{free moves (Lemma~\\ref{lem:F})} \\\\\n163\t3 & \\quad \\textbf{if} $S=C$ \\textbf{then return} true & \\\\\n164\t4 & \\quad \\textbf{if} $S$ is in the memo \\textbf{then return} false & \\textcolor{gray}{memo} \\\\\n165\t5 & \\quad $P\\gets$ the $c\\notin S\\cup Q$ with $\\cost(S,c)\\le k$, in index order & \\textcolor{gray}{cost cut} \\\\\n166\t6 & \\quad $L\\gets$ filter of $P$: definite move, then subset rule, then better move & \\textcolor{gray}{Sections~\\ref{sec:definite}--\\ref{sec:other-rules}} \\\\\n167\t7 & \\quad $\\mathit{seen}\\gets Q\\setminus S$ & \\\\\n168\t8 & \\quad \\textbf{for each} $c\\in L$: & \\\\\n169\t9 & \\quad\\quad $Q'\\gets\\{q\\in\\mathit{seen} : \\cost(S\\cup\\{q\\},c)\\le k\\}$ & \\textcolor{gray}{old moves} \\\\\n170\t10 & \\quad\\quad \\textbf{if} $\\textsc{Search}(S\\cup\\{c\\},Q')$ \\textbf{then return} true & \\\\\n171\t11 & \\quad\\quad $\\mathit{seen}\\gets\\mathit{seen}\\cup\\{c\\}$ & \\\\\n172\t12 & \\quad add $S$ to the memo & \\\\\n173\t13 & \\quad \\textbf{return} false & \\\\\n174\t\\end{tabular}\n175\t\\end{algorithm}\n176\t\n177\tLine~2 is safe by Lemma~\\ref{lem:F}, and the cost cut of line~5 is exact,\n178\tsince an unplayable move is not a move of $\\Sol_k$. The rest is pruning, and\n179\teach piece has a one-sentence intuition.\n180\t\\begin{itemize}\n181\t\\item \\emph{The memo} (lines~4 and~12): whether a state can be finished does\n182\t  not depend on how it was reached, so a state refuted once is refused when\n183\t  met again.\n184\t\\item \\emph{Old moves} (lines~7--11): a sibling whose subtree failed stays\n185\t  failed further down, as long as closing it first would have kept each later\n186\t  step within the bound.\n187\t\\item \\emph{The definite move}: if closing $q$ now leaves no more stacks open\n188\t  than before, it seems safe to close $q$ at once and try nothing else.\n189\t\\item \\emph{The subset rule}: if $d$ newly opens a subset of what $r$ newly\n190\t  opens, then $d$ is at least as good a move as $r$.\n191\t\\item \\emph{The better move}: if $q$ would be a definite move right after $r$,\n192\t  then closing $q$ before $r$ is at least as good as closing $r$ first.\n193\t\\end{itemize}\n194\tEach of the three filtering rules discards a move $r$ by citing a\n195\t\\emph{covering}, a kept move $c$ with\n196\t$\\Sol_k(S\\cdot r)\\Rightarrow\\Sol_k(S\\cdot c)$. The filter runs the definite\n197\tmove, then the subset rule, then the better move, each once. If the definite\n198\tmove fires, it keeps its customer alone.\n199\t\n200\tThis loop is the decision procedure of \\citet[Algorithm~1,\n201\tp.~393]{KobayashiKomuroTamaki2014}, which asks whether a vertex set extends to\n202\ta $w$-feasible permutation: replace the set by a committed extension, answer\n203\tfrom a failure table or at the full vertex set, recurse on each feasible\n204\tone-vertex extension, and record failures. The customer search is that loop\n205\twith $w=k-1$. The free closure and the definite move play the part of the\n206\tcommitment, the memo is the failure table, and the cost cut is the feasibility\n207\ttest. \\citet[\\S3.2]{CoudertMazauricNisse2014} has the same three parts in a\n208\tbranch and bound. Neither line cites the other in the papers we hold:\n209\t\\citet{ChuStuckey2009} predates the commitments and both 2014 papers, and none\n210\tof the pathwidth papers cites it. This is the isolation measured in\n211\tTable~\\ref{tab:communities}, at the level of one algorithm.\n212\t\n213\t\\subsection{The definite move is false as published, and its repair is a matching}\n214\t\\label{sec:definite}\n215\t\n216\tThe definite move is the strongest of the rules, since when it fires it\n217\tdiscards every other candidate. Its premise looks like a safe accounting:\n218\tclosing $q$ opens some new stacks and lets some customers close, and if it\n219\tlets at least as many close as it opens, nothing seems lost by closing it\n220\tfirst. \\citet[\\S3.1, p.~6]{ChuStuckey2009} state:\n221\t\\begin{quote}\n222\t\\textbf{Theorem 1.} Suppose S ++ [q] is playable and close(q, S) $\\ge$\n223\topen(q, S), then if U$'$ = S ++ R is a solution, there exists a solution\n224\tU = S ++ [q] ++ R$'$.\n225\t\\end{quote}\n226\tHere ++ is concatenation of closing sequences, a \\emph{solution} is a complete\n227\tclosing order with every step playable, and $\\close(q,S)$ counts the customers\n228\t$d$ with $o(d,S)\\subseteq o(q,S)$ (p.~4). The rule keeps $q$ alone. We read $d$\n229\tas ranging over customers not yet closed, as our implementation does; read\n230\tliterally, closed customers count too, which weakens the premise, and both\n231\treadings are refuted below. \\citet[Thm~6.3.6, p.~142]{Chu2011} restates the\n232\ttheorem with the same premise and proof, reworded for $k$ stacks. Write\n233\t$X=S\\cdot q$. The first lemma confirms that the premise says what the\n234\tintuition says.\n235\t\n236\t\\begin{lemma}[what the premise says]\\label{lem:premise}\n237\t$\\close(q,S)=|X\\setminus S|$. Hence the premise holds iff $b(X)\\le b(S)$: the\n238\tchild has no more open stacks than the parent~\\leanref{premise}.\n239\t\\end{lemma}\n240\t\n241\tThe premise compares only the two ends, $S$ and $X$. The rule fails because a\n242\tset between them can be cheaper still. Figure~\\ref{fig:cex}(a) shows the graph on\n243\twhich it fails, and panel~(b) the cheaper set between.\n244\t\n245\t\\begin{counterexample}\\label{cex:definite}\n246\tLet $G$ be the graph of Figure~\\ref{fig:cex}, on customers $0,\\dots,13$ with\n247\tthe 26 edges\n248\t\\begin{gather*}\n249\t0\\text{--}3,\\ 0\\text{--}4,\\ 0\\text{--}7,\\ 0\\text{--}11,\\ 1\\text{--}2,\\ 1\\text{--}5,\\ 1\\text{--}6,\\ 2\\text{--}3,\\ 2\\text{--}4,\\ 5\\text{--}9,\\ 5\\text{--}11,\\ 5\\text{--}12,\\ 6\\text{--}8,\\\\\n250\t6\\text{--}13,\\ 7\\text{--}9,\\ 7\\text{--}10,\\ 7\\text{--}13,\\ 8\\text{--}9,\\ 8\\text{--}10,\\ 8\\text{--}11,\\ 9\\text{--}10,\\ 9\\text{--}11,\\ 10\\text{--}11,\\ 10\\text{--}12,\\ 10\\text{--}13,\\ 12\\text{--}13,\n251\t\\end{gather*}\n252\twith $S=\\{2\\}$, $k=6$ and $q=0$. Then $O(S)=\\{1,2,3,4\\}$,\n253\t$o(0,S)=\\{0,7,11\\}$ and $\\cost(S,0)=6$, so $0$ is playable. The customers with\n254\t$o(d,S)\\subseteq\\{0,7,11\\}$ are $0$, $3$ and $4$, so $\\close(0,S)=3\\ge\\open(0,S)$,\n255\tand $0$ comes first in index order, so the rule keeps only $0$. A solution\n256\texists from $S$: the order\n257\t\\[1,3,4,6,12,13,0,5,7,8,9,10,11\\]\n258\tcosts at most 6. None exists from the child $X=\\{0,2,3,4\\}$: only seven sets\n259\tare reachable from $X$ by moves of cost at most 6, and none is $C$~\\leanref{cex}.\n260\t\\end{counterexample}\n261\t\n262\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n263\t\\centering\n264\t\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{definite_counterexample.pdf}\n265\t\\caption{The definite move fails because a set between parent and child is\n266\tcheaper than both. (a) The graph of Counterexample~\\ref{cex:definite} at the\n267\tstate where the rule fires. Customer 2 is closed; 1, 3 and 4 are open. Moving 0\n268\topens the new stacks 0, 7 and 11 and closes 0, 3 and 4, so open = close = 3 and\n269\tthe rule keeps 0 alone. But 3 and 4 have the single new stack 0 between them:\n270\tclosing them first gains two stacks for the price of one, which a solution\n271\tstarting with 0 cannot match. (b) The intermediate set $B=\\{2,3,4\\}$ leaves two\n272\tstacks open, fewer than the three of the child $X=\\{0,2,3,4\\}$, so the repaired\n273\trule of this section does not fire.}\n274\t\\label{fig:cex}\n275\t\\end{figure}\n276\t\n277\tThe published implication, as a universal statement, is refuted in Lean under\n278\tboth readings of $\\close$, and so is the thesis's Theorem~6.3.6~\\leanref{thm1false}.\n279\t\\citet[pp.~27--28]{Fink2012} restates the rule with a stronger premise,\n280\tcounting only dominated customers of smaller index than $q$. The graph above\n281\trefutes it under no labelling, but a third twin of 3 and 4 gives a\n282\t15-customer graph that does~\\leanref{fink}. The published proof moves $q$ to\n283\tthe front of a solution and counts, after each prefix, the stacks $q$ opens\n284\tagainst the customers it lets close. But customers that the solution has\n285\talready closed no longer count as closed by $q$, while the stacks $q$ opens may\n286\tstill be new. Appendix~\\ref{app:whyfails} gives the full analysis, and\n287\tAppendix~\\ref{app:minimality} the search for smaller counterexamples.\n288\t\n289\t\\paragraph{The repair.} The fix is to require the comparison at every set\n290\tbetween the two ends, not only at $S$. Call $q$ \\emph{hereditarily definite} at\n291\t$S$ when $b(X)\\le b(B)$ for every $B$ with $S\\subseteq B\\subseteq X$ and\n292\t$q\\notin B$. By Lemma~\\ref{lem:premise}, the published premise is the case\n293\t$B=S$. In the counterexample the repair fails at $B=\\{2,3,4\\}$\n294\t(Figure~\\ref{fig:cex}(b)), where\n295\t$b(B)=2<3=b(X)$~\\leanref{hered}. The proof that the repair is sound needs one\n296\tinequality.\n297\t\n298\t\\begin{lemma}[open stacks are submodular]\\label{lem:submod}\n299\tFor all $A,B\\subseteq C$, $b(A\\cup B)+b(A\\cap B)\\le b(A)+b(B)$~\\leanref{submod}.\n300\t\\end{lemma}\n301\t\\begin{proof}\n302\t$b(T)=|O(T)|-|T|$, $O(A\\cup B)=O(A)\\cup O(B)$ and $O(A\\cap B)\\subseteq\n303\tO(A)\\cap O(B)$; the customers themselves balance exactly.\n304\t\\end{proof}\n305\t\n306\t\\begin{theorem}[the repaired definite move is sound]\\label{thm:repaired}\n307\tIf $q\\notin S$ is hereditarily definite at $S$, then $P_k(S)\\Rightarrow P_k(X)$.\n308\tAt a state with the invariant, $\\Sol_k(S)\\Rightarrow\\Sol_k(S\\cdot q)$~\\leanref{repaired}.\n309\t\\end{theorem}\n310\t\\begin{proof}\n311\tTake an order from $S$ of cost at most $k$ with prefixes $T_i$, in which $q$ is\n312\tthe $j$-th step, and follow it from $X$, skipping customers already in $X$. A\n313\tstep $i<j$ closes $c_i$ at $T_{i-1}\\cup X$. We have\n314\t$S\\subseteq T_i\\cap X\\subseteq X$ and $q\\notin T_i$, so the hypothesis gives\n315\t$b(X)\\le b(T_i\\cap X)$, and Lemma~\\ref{lem:submod} gives\n316\t$b(T_i\\cup X)\\le b(T_i)$: the step costs no more than the original. After step\n317\t$j-1$ the closed set contains $T_j$ with the same opened set, and the rest of\n318\tthe order applies. The search's form follows by Lemma~\\ref{lem:F}.\n319\t\\end{proof}\n320\t\n321\tThis theorem is not new. The hereditary premise is the commitment condition of\n322\tTamaki, and the proof is the proof of the commitment lemma,\n323\t\\citet[Lemma~1, p.~142]{Kitsunai2016}; Section~\\ref{sec:layouts} makes the\n324\tcorrespondence exact.\n325\t\n326\t\\paragraph{The matching test.} The repaired rule seems to require checking\n327\texponentially many intermediate sets. It does not, and this cheap test is the\n328\talgorithmic contribution of the section; we did not find it in the papers we\n329\thold. In words: the rule is safe exactly when all but one of the new stacks\n330\tcan be paired with a different freed customer who needed it. In the\n331\tcounterexample, closing 0 opens three new stacks and frees 3 and 4, both of\n332\twhom needed only stack 0: one pairing where two are required.\n333\t\n334\t\\begin{theorem}[the repair is a matching condition]\\label{thm:matching}\n335\tLet $Y=X\\setminus(S\\cup\\{q\\})$, and join each $d\\in Y$ to the stacks of\n336\t$o(d,S)\\subseteq o(q,S)$. Then $q$ is hereditarily definite at $S$ iff this\n337\tbipartite graph has a matching with at least $\\open(q,S)-1$ edges~\\leanref{matching}.\n338\t\\end{theorem}\n339\t\\begin{proof}\n340\tThe sets of the repair are $B=S\\cup D$, $D\\subseteq Y$, and\n341\t$b(X)\\le b(S\\cup D)$ iff $|D|-|o(D)|\\le\\delta$ with\n342\t$\\delta=|Y|+1-\\open(q,S)$, where $o(D)$ is the union of the $o(d,S)$. By the\n343\tdeficiency form of Hall's theorem the largest matching has size\n344\t$|Y|-\\max_D(|D|-|o(D)|)$, which is at least $|Y|-\\delta=\\open(q,S)-1$ exactly\n345\twhen the repair holds.\n346\t\\end{proof}\n347\t\n348\tThe bipartite graph has at most $\\close-1$ customers on one side and $\\open$\n349\tstacks on the other, and the matching can stop at $\\open-1$ edges. Every $q$\n350\twith $\\open(q,S)\\le1$ passes at once, so the published rule is right\n351\tthere~\\leanref{open1}.\n352\t\n353\t\\subsection{The other rules}\n354\t\\label{sec:other-rules}\n355\t\n356\tEach of the remaining rules is quoted, proved sound in the form used, and\n357\tshown to need the test or tie-break it carries.\n358\t\n359\t\\paragraph{The subset rule.} ``If $o(c_i,S)\\subseteq o(c_j,S)$ and $i<j$ \\dots\\\n360\tmove $j$ can be removed'' \\citep[\\S2, p.~5]{ChuStuckey2009}. As implemented,\n361\t$d$ dominates $r$ when $o(d,S)\\subseteq o(r,S)$ and the inclusion is strict or\n362\t$d<r$. Every dominated playable $r$ is dropped, with a fallback that keeps $P$\n363\tif all would go. The covering is sound for every set $S$: if\n364\t$o(d,S)\\subseteq o(r,S)$ and $r$ is playable, then $d$ is playable and\n365\t$\\Sol_k(S\\cdot r)\\Rightarrow \\Sol_k(S\\cdot d)$, because $d$ is free after $r$\n366\tand closing $d$ then $r$ reaches the same state at no greater cost. With the\n367\tindex tie-break, domination is a strict order, so an undominated move with a\n368\tsolution survives, and the fallback never fires when a solution\n369\texists~\\leanref{subset}. The tie-break is necessary: without it, two customers\n370\twith equal new stacks dominate each other and both go, which loses a solution\n371\ton a 7-customer graph~\\leanref{tiebreak}.\n372\t\n373\t\\paragraph{The better move.} \\citet[\\S3.2, p.~6]{ChuStuckey2009}: ``Suppose\n374\tS ++ [q] and S ++ [r, q] are playable and close(q, S $\\cup$ \\{r\\}) $\\ge$\n375\topen(q, S $\\cup$ \\{r\\}) then if U$'$ = S ++ [r] ++ R is a solution there exists a\n376\tsolution U = S ++ [q] ++ R$'$.'' Its premise is the definite move's premise for\n377\t$q$ at the child $\\cl(S\\cup\\{r\\})$, together with playability of $r$ then $q$,\n378\tand its conclusion swaps $q$ and $r$~\\leanref{isbetter}. The swap is sound,\n379\tbecause both orders end at the same state and neither step overflows. The\n380\tappeal to the definite move is not.\n381\t\n382\t\\emph{Counterexample.} On the graph of Counterexample~\\ref{cex:definite} at the\n383\troot with $k=6$, the definite move does not fire, customers 0 and 2 both\n384\tsurvive the subset rule, and the premise holds for $r=2$, $q=0$, so the rule\n385\tdrops 2 citing 0. But $\\{2\\}$ has a solution and $\\{0\\}$ has\n386\tnone~\\leanref{bettercex}. At that node another kept customer still has a\n387\tsolution, so this is a false link and not a lost node; no node where the\n388\tbetter move loses the last solution has been found. \\citet[Thm~6.3.8,\n389\tp.~143]{Chu2011} states the better move with a different premise,\n390\t$\\close(q,S)\\ge\\open(q,S\\cup\\{r\\})$, and proves it through his Theorem~6.3.6.\n391\tThe same graph refutes it at $S=\\{2\\}$, $r=3$, $q=0$~\\leanref{thm638}.\n392\t\n393\t\\emph{Repair and test.} Cite $q$ for $r$ only if $q$ is hereditarily definite\n394\tat $\\cl(S\\cup\\{r\\})$, which is one matching at the child\n395\t(Theorem~\\ref{thm:matching}); then the swap argument goes\n396\tthrough~\\leanref{repbetter}.\n397\t\n398\t\\emph{Two requirements.} The close count in the premise must not include the\n399\tcustomers that $r$ finishes on its own; counting them makes the rule's own\n400\tconclusion false on a 12-customer graph. And the rules must run in the order\n401\tdefinite, subset, better, each citing only customers still standing: if the\n402\tbetter move runs first, the subset rule can cite a customer the better move\n403\thas already dropped, and an 8-customer graph loses a node through a cycle of\n404\tlinks that are each true~\\leanref{bugs}. The second is an instance of the\n405\tgeneral observation of \\citet[Prop.~3, Example~6]{Beck2025} that mutually\n406\tdominating transitions must be tie-broken.\n407\t\n408\t\\paragraph{The old move.} \\citet[Thm~3, p.~7]{ChuStuckey2009} let a child\n409\tinherit a sibling $q$ whose subtree failed, provided that inserting $q$ before\n410\teach later step keeps that step playable (line~9 of\n411\tAlgorithm~\\ref{alg:search}). The rule is sound as implemented: if\n412\t$\\cost(S\\cup\\{q\\},c)\\le k$, then\n413\t$\\Sol_k(\\cl(\\{q\\}\\cup S\\cdot c))\\Rightarrow\\Sol_k(S\\cdot q)$, so a refuted\n414\tsibling stays refuted along any path whose steps pass the test~\\leanref{oldmove}.\n415\tThe test is necessary; the smallest graph that shows it is the path on five\n416\tvertices~\\leanref{reinsert}.\n417\t\n418\t\\paragraph{The memo.} Whether a state with $b(S)\\le k$ has a solution depends\n419\ton the state alone, not on the path that reached it~\\leanref{memo}. One\n420\tconcern is that the memo and the old move might not combine: a refutation\n421\tobtained with inherited old moves seems to depend on the ancestors, and one\n422\timplementation of the search declines to run both for that reason. A node\n423\ttaken on its own, with an old move that is not in fact refuted, can indeed\n424\trecord a solvable state~\\leanref{fakeold}. Theorem~\\ref{thm:runs}\n425\t(Appendix~\\ref{app:machinery}) shows that this never happens in a real run,\n426\tbecause every old move is a refutation completed earlier in the same run.\n427\t\n428\t\\subsection{The repaired search is sound}\n429\t\\label{sec:theorem}\n430\t\n431\t\\paragraph{What a rule must satisfy.} At a state $S$ with old moves $Q$, the\n432\tcost cut keeps the playable candidates $P$ and the filter keeps a sublist\n433\t$L\\subseteq P$. The filter is \\emph{node-sound} at $(S,Q)$ if, whenever every\n434\t$q\\in Q$ has $\\neg\\Sol_k(S\\cdot q)$, $\\Sol_k(S)$ implies $\\Sol_k(S\\cdot c)$ for\n435\tsome $c\\in L$~\\leanref{nodesound}. In terms of coverings, a node is sound when\n436\ttwo things hold. First, every link a rule cites is a true covering. Second,\n437\tevery chain of links from a discarded candidate ends in $L$ or in $Q$. The\n438\tsecond condition can fail through a cycle even when every link is true, which\n439\tis why the order of the rules matters. Proposition~\\ref{prop:filter} shows that\n440\tthe repaired filter is node-sound, and Theorem~\\ref{thm:runs} that node\n441\tsoundness, the memo and the old move together give sound runs; both are in\n442\tAppendix~\\ref{app:machinery}. Together they give the main theorem.\n443\t\n444\t\\begin{theorem}[the revised search is sound]\\label{thm:main}\n445\tRun the customer search with free moves, the memo, old moves and the filter\n446\tdefinite $\\to$ subset $\\to$ better, the definite move firing only on a\n447\thereditarily definite customer and the better move citing only under the\n448\trepaired premise. If it refutes $k$ from the root, then no closing order costs\n449\tat most $k$, $\\pw(G)>k-1$, and on the MOSP graph of an instance $M$ with at\n450\tleast one 1, $Z(M)>k$~\\leanref{main}.\n451\t\\end{theorem}\n452\t\n453\tIn words: each rule throws away only moves that a kept move is proved to be at\n454\tleast as good as, and the memo and the old moves remember only failures that\n455\treally happened earlier in the same search. So when the search says ``no'',\n456\tno closing order fits in $k$ stacks.\n457\t\n458\t\\paragraph{What is proved and what is tested.} The theorem is about the search\n459\tas specified; that our C and Python implementations run it is checked, not\n460\tproved. Three checks link them:\n461\t\\begin{itemize}\n462\t\\item a transcription of the Lean filter agreed with the implemented filter on\n463\t  16,244,090 node checks over graphs on 1 to 17 vertices;\n464\t\\item the matching agreed with the hereditary premise on 36,939,226 candidates;\n465\t\\item a search written from the theorems agreed with both implementations and\n466\t  an exact oracle on 17,431,232 runs over 52,592 graphs on 1 to 17 vertices.\n467\t\\end{itemize}\n468\tSection~\\ref{sec:certs} adds a certificate checker for individual\n469\trefutations.\n470\t\n471\t\\paragraph{The published rules.} Under the published rules the filter is not\n472\tnode-sound~\\leanref{notsound}. In a check of 570,206 runs with memo and old\n473\tmove, it lost the last solution at a node in 58 runs, all on graphs built\n474\taround Counterexample~\\ref{cex:definite} and all at $k$ equal to the optimum.\n475\tEvery one found a solution through another branch and answered correctly. No\n476\twhole instance on which the published rules answer \\emph{unsat} wrongly is\n477\tknown.\n478\t\n479\t\\subsection{The rules in pathwidth language}\n480\t\\label{sec:layouts}\n481\t\n482\tThe definite move has an exact counterpart in the pathwidth literature, and\n483\tthis subsection makes the correspondence precise.\n484\t\\citet[p.~142]{Kitsunai2016}, stating Tamaki's notion, call an extension $\\tau$\n485\tof a vertex sequence $\\sigma$ \\emph{committable} when\n486\t$|\\partial X'|\\ge|\\partial V(\\tau)|$ for every $X'$ with\n487\t$V(\\sigma)\\subseteq X'\\subseteq V(\\tau)$. Their Lemma~1 says that if $\\sigma$\n488\textends to a feasible permutation, so does $\\tau$.\n489\t\n490\t\\begin{lemma}\\label{lem:endpoint}\n491\tFor $q\\notin S$ and $X=\\cl(S\\cup\\{q\\})$: the published premise holds iff\n492\t$b(X)\\le b(S)$, the commitment condition at the single set $X'=S$; and the\n493\trepaired premise holds iff $X$ is a commitment from $S$, the condition at every\n494\t$X'$ between them~\\leanref{endpoint}.\n495\t\\end{lemma}\n496\t\n497\tSo Theorem~\\ref{thm:repaired} is the commitment lemma at the child, and the\n498\tpublished definite move is a commitment whose interior condition was dropped.\n499\tIn Counterexample~\\ref{cex:definite} the endpoint test passes with\n500\t$b(X)=3=b(S)$, while the interior set $\\{2,3,4\\}$ has border\n501\t2~\\leanref{cexnotcommit}. The published premise is not wasted: by\n502\t\\citet[Lemma~10, Cor.~2, pp.~148--149]{Kitsunai2016} it guarantees a\n503\tcommitment to the least-border set between $S$ and $X$, which need not contain\n504\t$q$~\\leanref{existscommit}. In the counterexample that set is\n505\t$\\{2,3,4\\}$~\\leanref{commit234}. Committing there when the matching fails is a\n506\tsecond repair that gives up no pruning; we have not built it.\n507\t\n508\tA commitment of depth one, $S$ to $S\\cup\\{q\\}$, holds iff\n509\t$\\open(q,S)\\le1$~\\leanref{depth1}. The greedy step of\n510\t\\citet[Lemma~3, p.~49]{CoudertMazauricNisse2014} is exactly that\n511\tcondition~\\leanref{greedy}, and the push rule of\n512\t\\citet[Rule~1, p.~330]{SuchanVillanger2009} is a case of it. So every published\n513\tpathwidth rule of this kind lies in the region $\\open\\le1$, where the published\n514\tdefinite move is right; \\citet[p.~388, Table~2]{KobayashiKomuroTamaki2014}\n515\treport depth-one commitments as ``extremely effective''. Chu and Stuckey's\n516\tdefinite move reaches into $\\open\\ge2$, and that is where it fails. For the\n517\tcost of the test, \\citet[Cor.~2]{Kitsunai2016} find the least border between\n518\tthe ends of a given extension by a minimum $s$--$t$ separator;\n519\tTheorem~\\ref{thm:matching} is that computation for the single target $X$, where\n520\tit becomes a bipartite matching.\n521\t\n522\tTable~\\ref{tab:known} sums up, and its rows are grouped by what they say. The\n523\tpublished definite and better moves are false. The free move, the memo and\n524\tthe repaired definite move are known pathwidth results. The subset rule, the repaired\n525\tbetter move and the old move have no counterpart we found, and are proved\n526\there, as is the matching test. The comparison is limited to the pathwidth\n527\tpapers we hold\n528\t\\citep{SuchanVillanger2009,Bodlaender2012,CoudertMazauricNisse2014,CoudertMazauricNisse2016,KobayashiKomuroTamaki2014,Kitsunai2016};\n529\t``none found'' is not a claim of priority.\n530\t\n531\t\\begin{table}[t]\n532\t\\centering\\small\n533\t\\caption{Two published rules are false; the free move, the memo and the\n534\trepaired definite move are known pathwidth results; the subset rule, the\n535\trepaired better move and the old move, like the matching test, are proved here.\n536\tEach rule of the customer search against the exact-pathwidth literature.}\n537\t\\label{tab:known}\n538\t\\begin{tabularx}{\\linewidth}{@{}>{\\raggedright}p{3.2cm}>{\\raggedright}X>{\\raggedright\\arraybackslash}p{4.1cm}@{}}\n539\t\\toprule\n540\tRule & Published counterpart & Status \\\\\n541\t\\midrule\n542\tdefinite move, published & -- & false (Cex.~\\ref{cex:definite}) \\\\\n543\tbetter move, published & none found & false \\\\\n544\t\\midrule\n545\tfree move & full sets \\citep{SuchanVillanger2009}; \\citet[Prop.~3]{Kitsunai2016} & known \\\\\n546\tdefinite move, $\\open\\le1$ & depth-one commitments; greedy step; push rule & known \\\\\n547\tdefinite move, repaired & commitment lemma \\citep[Lemma~1]{Kitsunai2016} & known; matching test (Thm~\\ref{thm:matching}) not found \\\\\n548\tmemo & subset DP \\citep{Bodlaender2012}; prefix and failure tables & known; with the old move, proved here (Thm~\\ref{thm:runs}) \\\\\n549\t\\midrule\n550\tsubset rule & none found & proved here \\\\\n551\tbetter move, repaired & none found & proved here \\\\\n552\told move & none found & proved here \\\\\n553\t\\bottomrule\n554\t\\end{tabularx}\n555\t\\end{table}\n556\t\n557\t\\paragraph{Prior reports.} We found no earlier report of the error in the 56\n558\tworks we read in full of the 141 that cite the customer search's papers\n559\t(Appendix~\\ref{app:prior}). The pathwidth literature has the correct form of\n560\tthe rule, as a commitment, and none of the pathwidth papers we hold mentions\n561\tMOSP.\n562\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b19c00ce-b033-4bb1-a352-ad2cf0b0fbf8","uuid":"4bc32b90-32d6-4030-b435-355b691bd993","timestamp":"2026-10-06T18:43:49.033Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/al/dev/MOSP/paper2/latex/sec4_search.tex","content":"% Section 4. Sources: paper2/revised_algorithm.md (4.1-4.6), search_soundness.md,\n% prior_art_counterexample.md (prior art). Lean: lean/MOSPFormalization/Search/,\n% names in Appendix A (sec9a_appendix_lean.tex). The old 4.6-4.7 are Section 5.\n% Restructured 2026-10-06 (loop0009 item 05, review changes 4 and 5): stakes\n% first, the pathwidth reading and the dictionary in 4.1, Algorithm 4.1 in 4.2,\n% each rule as intuition -> counterexample -> repair -> test.\n% Page numbers for Chu & Stuckey (2009) are those of the preprint\n% literature/chu_stuckey_2009.pdf; for Chu (2011) the printed ones.\n\\section{An exact open-stacks search, read as a pathwidth algorithm}\n\\label{sec:search}\n\nIf MOSP is pathwidth (Theorem~\\ref{thm:mosp}), then the best exact MOSP\nalgorithm is a pathwidth algorithm, and its rules can be checked against what\nthe pathwidth literature knows. This section does that for the \\emph{customer\nsearch} of \\citet{ChuStuckey2009}, the search that Section~\\ref{sec:names}\ntraced from the open-stacks challenge. It searches over the orders in which the\ncustomers' stacks close, not over pattern sequences, and it prunes with\ndominance rules. Three of the rules are stated as theorems (their Theorems~1,\n2 and~3) and a fourth, the subset rule, in their \\S2. \\citet{ChuStuckey2009}\nplace MOSP among ``graph path-width and gate matrix layout'', citing\n\\citet{LY2002}, but they do not use pathwidth in the search. Any graph is the\nMOSP graph of the instance with one customer per vertex and one pattern per\nedge, so the search decides pathwidth for every graph.\n\nThe stakes are these. Two of the published dominance theorems, the\n\\emph{definite move} and the \\emph{better move}, are false, and so are their\nrestatements in Chu's thesis \\citep{Chu2011} and, with a stronger premise, in\n\\citet{Fink2012}. Yet no wrong answer is known: no whole instance on which the\npublished rules answer ``no'' wrongly has been found, and when every benchmark\noptimum that rested on the search was proved again with repaired rules, no\nvalue changed (Section~\\ref{sec:results}). The correct form of the definite\nmove was already known for pathwidth, as Tamaki's commitment lemma, and we\nshow that it can be tested by one small bipartite matching per candidate. The\nmain result, Theorem~\\ref{thm:main}, says informally: \\emph{with both rules\nrepaired, the search is sound; whenever it answers that $k$ stacks do not\nsuffice, they do not, and the graph has pathwidth at least $k$.} The repair\ncosts at most 2\\% more search nodes on each benchmark set\n(Section~\\ref{sec:results}).\n\nSection~\\ref{sec:search-def} defines the search and shows that it decides\npathwidth, in both vocabularies. Section~\\ref{sec:algorithm} gives the\nalgorithm. Sections~\\ref{sec:definite} and~\\ref{sec:other-rules} take the\nrules one at a time, Section~\\ref{sec:theorem} proves the repaired search\nsound, and Section~\\ref{sec:layouts} places each rule in the pathwidth\nliterature.\n\n\\subsection{The search decides pathwidth}\n\\label{sec:search-def}\n\n\\citet[p.~4]{ChuStuckey2009} observe that the patterns matter only through the\nMOSP graph. So fix a graph $G=(C,E)$ whose vertices are the customers, and an\ninteger $k\\ge0$. In this section $k$ bounds the number of open stacks, which is\none more than a width: $k$ stacks suffice exactly when the pathwidth is at most\n$k-1$ (Theorem~\\ref{thm:decides}). For a customer $c$ write $N[c]$ for its\nclosed neighbourhood, the stacks that must be open when $c$'s stack closes. For\na set $T\\subseteq C$ of vertices, its \\emph{border} $\\partial T$ is the set of\nvertices outside $T$ with a neighbour in $T$. For a set $T$ of closed\ncustomers write\n\\begin{align*}\nO(T) &= \\textstyle\\bigcup_{c\\in T}N[c] && \\text{the stacks opened once $T$ has closed},\\\\\n\\fin(X) &= \\{c : N[c]\\subseteq X\\} && \\text{the customers free to close when $X$ is open},\\\\\n\\cl(T) &= \\fin(O(T)) && \\text{the \\emph{free closure} of $T$},\\\\\nb(T) &= |O(T)\\setminus T| = |\\partial T| && \\text{the stacks open after $T$ has closed},\\\\\no(c,T) &= N[c]\\setminus O(T),\\quad \\open(c,T)=|o(c,T)| && \\text{the stacks closing $c$ would newly open.}\n\\end{align*}\nThe open stacks after $T$ has closed are exactly the border of $T$, and this\none identity carries the whole translation to pathwidth.\n\nA \\emph{move} at $T$ closes one customer $c\\notin T$, at cost\n$\\cost(T,c)=|O(T\\cup\\{c\\})\\setminus T| = b(T\\cup\\{c\\})+1$, the stacks open at\nthat moment counting $c$. It is \\emph{playable} when $\\cost(T,c)\\le k$. Write\n$P_k(T)$ when some order of $C\\setminus T$ has every step playable. On entering\na node the search closes every free customer, so it stands only at\n\\emph{states}, sets with $S=\\cl(S)$. The \\emph{child} of $S$ by $c$ is\n$S\\cdot c=\\cl(S\\cup\\{c\\})$. The search's predicate $\\Sol_k(S)$ holds when\n$S=C$, or when some playable $c$ has $\\Sol_k(S\\cdot c)$~\\leanref{defs}. Every\nnode the search visits satisfies the invariant $b(S)\\le k$.\n\n\\paragraph{An example.} Take the graph of Figure~\\ref{fig:example}(d), with\n$k=3$. At $T=\\{a\\}$ we have $O(T)=\\{a,b\\}$, so one stack, $b$, is open:\n$b(T)=1$ and $\\partial T=\\{b\\}$. No other customer is free, since $N[b]$\ncontains $c$ and $d$, so $T$ is a state. Closing $b$ newly opens\n$o(b,T)=\\{c,d\\}$ and costs $\\cost(T,b)=3$, the stacks $b$, $c$ and $d$; the\nmove is playable. At $\\{a,b,c\\}$ every neighbour of $d$ is open, so $d$ is\nfree, and the child $\\{a,b\\}\\cdot c$ is $\\{a,b,c,d\\}$. The closing order\n$a,b,c,e$ (with $d$ and $f$ closed free) costs $2,3,3,2$, the profile of\norder~A in Figure~\\ref{fig:example}(a).\n\n\\begin{lemma}[free moves never hurt]\\label{lem:F}\nIf $b(T)\\le k$, then $P_k(T)\\iff\\Sol_k(\\cl(T))$~\\leanref{F}.\n\\end{lemma}\nClosing a customer whose neighbourhood is already open opens nothing, never\nmakes a later step dearer, and leaves one fewer stack open. The hypothesis is\nneeded: closing the free customers one by one costs $b(T)$ at each step.\n\n\\begin{theorem}[the search decides pathwidth]\\label{thm:decides}\nFor nonempty $C$, $\\Sol_k(\\emptyset)\\iff\\pw(G)+1\\le k$. On the MOSP graph of an\ninstance $M$ with at least one 1, $\\Sol_k(\\emptyset)\\iff Z(M)\\le k$~\\leanref{decides}.\n\\end{theorem}\n\\begin{proof}\nFor a closing order $c_1,\\dots,c_n$ with prefixes $S_i$, the $i$-th step costs\n$1+|\\partial S_i|$. Reverse the order to get a layout. Its first $n-i$\nvertices are $C\\setminus S_i$, and those with a neighbour among the remaining\nones are exactly $\\partial S_i$. So the cost of the closing order is the vertex\nseparation of the reversed layout plus one, and Lemma~\\ref{lem:F} at the root\nand Theorem~\\ref{thm:vs} finish the proof.\n\\end{proof}\n\nSo a refutation, $\\neg\\Sol_k(\\emptyset)$, may only mean $\\pw(G)>k-1$. The rest\nof the section asks whether the pruning rules preserve that meaning.\n\n\\paragraph{In pathwidth language.} The exact pathwidth literature writes\n$N(T)$ for the border and $d(T)$ for its size\n\\citep[p.~390]{KobayashiKomuroTamaki2014}; we keep $\\partial T$ and $b(T)$. A\nvertex sequence is \\emph{$w$-feasible} when every prefix has border at most\n$w$, and $\\vs(G)$ is the least $w$ for which some permutation is $w$-feasible.\nTable~\\ref{tab:dictionary} translates each notion of the search into this\nvocabulary. Read it row by row: every ingredient of the customer search is a\nknown ingredient of an exact pathwidth algorithm, and the last two rows are\nthe rules of Section~\\ref{sec:algorithm}.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\\small\n\\caption{The customer search is, ingredient by ingredient, an exact pathwidth\nalgorithm: each notion of the search beside its name in the pathwidth\nliterature. The memo and the definite move are described in\nSection~\\ref{sec:algorithm}.}\n\\label{tab:dictionary}\n\\begin{tabularx}{\\linewidth}{@{}>{\\raggedright}p{5.2cm}>{\\raggedright\\arraybackslash}X@{}}\n\\toprule\nCustomer search & Layout \\\\\n\\midrule\nclosing order from $\\emptyset$ & vertex sequence, read forwards \\\\\nopen stacks $b(T)$ & border size $|\\partial T|$ \\\\\n$\\cost(T,c)$ & $|\\partial(T\\cup\\{c\\})|+1$ \\\\\n$\\Sol_k(\\emptyset)$ & $\\vs(G)\\le k-1$ \\\\\nfree closure $\\cl(T)$ & full set of $T$ \\citep[p.~328]{SuchanVillanger2009} \\\\\nmemo & failure table \\\\\ndefinite move, repaired & commitment to the child \\\\\n\\bottomrule\\end{tabularx}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{The algorithm}\n\\label{sec:algorithm}\n\nAlgorithm~\\ref{alg:search} gives the customer search. It is a depth-first\nsearch over states. At each node it closes the free customers, consults a\ntable of states already refuted, keeps the playable candidates, filters them\nwith the dominance rules and recurses on the survivors.\n\n\\begin{algorithm}[t]\n\\caption{The customer search \\citep{ChuStuckey2009} is a plain depth-first\nsearch; all its pruning sits in the memo (lines~4 and~12), the filter\n(line~6) and the old moves (line~9). It decides $\\Sol_k(S)$ for a graph\n$G=(C,E)$ and a stack bound $k$, and is called as\n$\\textsc{Search}(\\emptyset,\\emptyset)$. $Q$ is the set of old moves the node\ninherits.}\n\\label{alg:search}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}r@{\\quad}l@{\\qquad}l@{}}\n1 & $\\textsc{Search}(S,Q)$: & \\\\\n2 & \\quad $S\\gets\\cl(S)$ & \\textcolor{gray}{free moves (Lemma~\\ref{lem:F})} \\\\\n3 & \\quad \\textbf{if} $S=C$ \\textbf{then return} true & \\\\\n4 & \\quad \\textbf{if} $S$ is in the memo \\textbf{then return} false & \\textcolor{gray}{memo} \\\\\n5 & \\quad $P\\gets$ the $c\\notin S\\cup Q$ with $\\cost(S,c)\\le k$, in index order & \\textcolor{gray}{cost cut} \\\\\n6 & \\quad $L\\gets$ filter of $P$: definite move, then subset rule, then better move & \\textcolor{gray}{Sections~\\ref{sec:definite}--\\ref{sec:other-rules}} \\\\\n7 & \\quad $\\mathit{seen}\\gets Q\\setminus S$ & \\\\\n8 & \\quad \\textbf{for each} $c\\in L$: & \\\\\n9 & \\quad\\quad $Q'\\gets\\{q\\in\\mathit{seen} : \\cost(S\\cup\\{q\\},c)\\le k\\}$ & \\textcolor{gray}{old moves} \\\\\n10 & \\quad\\quad \\textbf{if} $\\textsc{Search}(S\\cup\\{c\\},Q')$ \\textbf{then return} true & \\\\\n11 & \\quad\\quad $\\mathit{seen}\\gets\\mathit{seen}\\cup\\{c\\}$ & \\\\\n12 & \\quad add $S$ to the memo & \\\\\n13 & \\quad \\textbf{return} false & \\\\\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{algorithm}\n\nLine~2 is safe by Lemma~\\ref{lem:F}, and the cost cut of line~5 is exact,\nsince an unplayable move is not a move of $\\Sol_k$. The rest is pruning, and\neach piece has a one-sentence intuition.\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\emph{The memo} (lines~4 and~12): whether a state can be finished does\n  not depend on how it was reached, so a state refuted once is refused when\n  met again.\n\\item \\emph{Old moves} (lines~7--11): a sibling whose subtree failed stays\n  failed further down, as long as closing it first would have kept each later\n  step within the bound.\n\\item \\emph{The definite move}: if closing $q$ now leaves no more stacks open\n  than before, it seems safe to close $q$ at once and try nothing else.\n\\item \\emph{The subset rule}: if $d$ newly opens a subset of what $r$ newly\n  opens, then $d$ is at least as good a move as $r$.\n\\item \\emph{The better move}: if $q$ would be a definite move right after $r$,\n  then closing $q$ before $r$ is at least as good as closing $r$ first.\n\\end{itemize}\nEach of the three filtering rules discards a move $r$ by citing a\n\\emph{covering}, a kept move $c$ with\n$\\Sol_k(S\\cdot r)\\Rightarrow\\Sol_k(S\\cdot c)$. The filter runs the definite\nmove, then the subset rule, then the better move, each once. If the definite\nmove fires, it keeps its customer alone.\n\nThis loop is the decision procedure of \\citet[Algorithm~1,\np.~393]{KobayashiKomuroTamaki2014}, which asks whether a vertex set extends to\na $w$-feasible permutation: replace the set by a committed extension, answer\nfrom a failure table or at the full vertex set, recurse on each feasible\none-vertex extension, and record failures. The customer search is that loop\nwith $w=k-1$. The free closure and the definite move play the part of the\ncommitment, the memo is the failure table, and the cost cut is the feasibility\ntest. \\citet[\\S3.2]{CoudertMazauricNisse2014} has the same three parts in a\nbranch and bound. Neither line cites the other in the papers we hold:\n\\citet{ChuStuckey2009} predates the commitments and both 2014 papers, and none\nof the pathwidth papers cites it. This is the isolation measured in\nTable~\\ref{tab:communities}, at the level of one algorithm.\n\n\\subsection{The definite move is false as published, and its repair is a matching}\n\\label{sec:definite}\n\nThe definite move is the strongest of the rules, since when it fires it\ndiscards every other candidate. Its premise looks like a safe accounting:\nclosing $q$ opens some new stacks and lets some customers close, and if it\nlets at least as many close as it opens, nothing seems lost by closing it\nfirst. \\citet[\\S3.1, p.~6]{ChuStuckey2009} state:\n\\begin{quote}\n\\textbf{Theorem 1.} Suppose S ++ [q] is playable and close(q, S) $\\ge$\nopen(q, S), then if U$'$ = S ++ R is a solution, there exists a solution\nU = S ++ [q] ++ R$'$.\n\\end{quote}\nHere ++ is concatenation of closing sequences, a \\emph{solution} is a complete\nclosing order with every step playable, and $\\close(q,S)$ counts the customers\n$d$ with $o(d,S)\\subseteq o(q,S)$ (p.~4). The rule keeps $q$ alone. We read $d$\nas ranging over customers not yet closed, as our implementation does; read\nliterally, closed customers count too, which weakens the premise, and both\nreadings are refuted below. \\citet[Thm~6.3.6, p.~142]{Chu2011} restates the\ntheorem with the same premise and proof, reworded for $k$ stacks. Write\n$X=S\\cdot q$. The first lemma confirms that the premise says what the\nintuition says.\n\n\\begin{lemma}[what the premise says]\\label{lem:premise}\n$\\close(q,S)=|X\\setminus S|$. Hence the premise holds iff $b(X)\\le b(S)$: the\nchild has no more open stacks than the parent~\\leanref{premise}.\n\\end{lemma}\n\nThe premise compares only the two ends, $S$ and $X$. The rule fails because a\nset between them can be cheaper still. Figure~\\ref{fig:cex}(a) shows the graph on\nwhich it fails, and panel~(b) the cheaper set between.\n\n\\begin{counterexample}\\label{cex:definite}\nLet $G$ be the graph of Figure~\\ref{fig:cex}, on customers $0,\\dots,13$ with\nthe 26 edges\n\\begin{gather*}\n0\\text{--}3,\\ 0\\text{--}4,\\ 0\\text{--}7,\\ 0\\text{--}11,\\ 1\\text{--}2,\\ 1\\text{--}5,\\ 1\\text{--}6,\\ 2\\text{--}3,\\ 2\\text{--}4,\\ 5\\text{--}9,\\ 5\\text{--}11,\\ 5\\text{--}12,\\ 6\\text{--}8,\\\\\n6\\text{--}13,\\ 7\\text{--}9,\\ 7\\text{--}10,\\ 7\\text{--}13,\\ 8\\text{--}9,\\ 8\\text{--}10,\\ 8\\text{--}11,\\ 9\\text{--}10,\\ 9\\text{--}11,\\ 10\\text{--}11,\\ 10\\text{--}12,\\ 10\\text{--}13,\\ 12\\text{--}13,\n\\end{gather*}\nwith $S=\\{2\\}$, $k=6$ and $q=0$. Then $O(S)=\\{1,2,3,4\\}$,\n$o(0,S)=\\{0,7,11\\}$ and $\\cost(S,0)=6$, so $0$ is playable. The customers with\n$o(d,S)\\subseteq\\{0,7,11\\}$ are $0$, $3$ and $4$, so $\\close(0,S)=3\\ge\\open(0,S)$,\nand $0$ comes first in index order, so the rule keeps only $0$. A solution\nexists from $S$: the order\n\\[1,3,4,6,12,13,0,5,7,8,9,10,11\\]\ncosts at most 6. None exists from the child $X=\\{0,2,3,4\\}$: only seven sets\nare reachable from $X$ by moves of cost at most 6, and none is $C$~\\leanref{cex}.\n\\end{counterexample}\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{definite_counterexample.pdf}\n\\caption{The definite move fails because a set between parent and child is\ncheaper than both. (a) The graph of Counterexample~\\ref{cex:definite} at the\nstate where the rule fires. Customer 2 is closed; 1, 3 and 4 are open. Moving 0\nopens the new stacks 0, 7 and 11 and closes 0, 3 and 4, so open = close = 3 and\nthe rule keeps 0 alone. But 3 and 4 have the single new stack 0 between them:\nclosing them first gains two stacks for the price of one, which a solution\nstarting with 0 cannot match. (b) The intermediate set $B=\\{2,3,4\\}$ leaves two\nstacks open, fewer than the three of the child $X=\\{0,2,3,4\\}$, so the repaired\nrule of this section does not fire.}\n\\label{fig:cex}\n\\end{figure}\n\nThe published implication, as a universal statement, is refuted in Lean under\nboth readings of $\\close$, and so is the thesis's Theorem~6.3.6~\\leanref{thm1false}.\n\\citet[pp.~27--28]{Fink2012} restates the rule with a stronger premise,\ncounting only dominated customers of smaller index than $q$. The graph above\nrefutes it under no labelling, but a third twin of 3 and 4 gives a\n15-customer graph that does~\\leanref{fink}. The published proof moves $q$ to\nthe front of a solution and counts, after each prefix, the stacks $q$ opens\nagainst the customers it lets close. But customers that the solution has\nalready closed no longer count as closed by $q$, while the stacks $q$ opens may\nstill be new. Appendix~\\ref{app:whyfails} gives the full analysis, and\nAppendix~\\ref{app:minimality} the search for smaller counterexamples.\n\n\\paragraph{The repair.} The fix is to require the comparison at every set\nbetween the two ends, not only at $S$. Call $q$ \\emph{hereditarily definite} at\n$S$ when $b(X)\\le b(B)$ for every $B$ with $S\\subseteq B\\subseteq X$ and\n$q\\notin B$. By Lemma~\\ref{lem:premise}, the published premise is the case\n$B=S$. In the counterexample the repair fails at $B=\\{2,3,4\\}$\n(Figure~\\ref{fig:cex}(b)), where\n$b(B)=2<3=b(X)$~\\leanref{hered}. The proof that the repair is sound needs one\ninequality.\n\n\\begin{lemma}[open stacks are submodular]\\label{lem:submod}\nFor all $A,B\\subseteq C$, $b(A\\cup B)+b(A\\cap B)\\le b(A)+b(B)$~\\leanref{submod}.\n\\end{lemma}\n\\begin{proof}\n$b(T)=|O(T)|-|T|$, $O(A\\cup B)=O(A)\\cup O(B)$ and $O(A\\cap B)\\subseteq\nO(A)\\cap O(B)$; the customers themselves balance exactly.\n\\end{proof}\n\n\\begin{theorem}[the repaired definite move is sound]\\label{thm:repaired}\nIf $q\\notin S$ is hereditarily definite at $S$, then $P_k(S)\\Rightarrow P_k(X)$.\nAt a state with the invariant, $\\Sol_k(S)\\Rightarrow\\Sol_k(S\\cdot q)$~\\leanref{repaired}.\n\\end{theorem}\n\\begin{proof}\nTake an order from $S$ of cost at most $k$ with prefixes $T_i$, in which $q$ is\nthe $j$-th step, and follow it from $X$, skipping customers already in $X$. A\nstep $i<j$ closes $c_i$ at $T_{i-1}\\cup X$. We have\n$S\\subseteq T_i\\cap X\\subseteq X$ and $q\\notin T_i$, so the hypothesis gives\n$b(X)\\le b(T_i\\cap X)$, and Lemma~\\ref{lem:submod} gives\n$b(T_i\\cup X)\\le b(T_i)$: the step costs no more than the original. After step\n$j-1$ the closed set contains $T_j$ with the same opened set, and the rest of\nthe order applies. The search's form follows by Lemma~\\ref{lem:F}.\n\\end{proof}\n\nThis theorem is not new. The hereditary premise is the commitment condition of\nTamaki, and the proof is the proof of the commitment lemma,\n\\citet[Lemma~1, p.~142]{Kitsunai2016}; Section~\\ref{sec:layouts} makes the\ncorrespondence exact.\n\n\\paragraph{The matching test.} The repaired rule seems to require checking\nexponentially many intermediate sets. It does not, and this cheap test is the\nalgorithmic contribution of the section; we did not find it in the papers we\nhold. In words: the rule is safe exactly when all but one of the new stacks\ncan be paired with a different freed customer who needed it. In the\ncounterexample, closing 0 opens three new stacks and frees 3 and 4, both of\nwhom needed only stack 0: one pairing where two are required.\n\n\\begin{theorem}[the repair is a matching condition]\\label{thm:matching}\nLet $Y=X\\setminus(S\\cup\\{q\\})$, and join each $d\\in Y$ to the stacks of\n$o(d,S)\\subseteq o(q,S)$. Then $q$ is hereditarily definite at $S$ iff this\nbipartite graph has a matching with at least $\\open(q,S)-1$ edges~\\leanref{matching}.\n\\end{theorem}\n\\begin{proof}\nThe sets of the repair are $B=S\\cup D$, $D\\subseteq Y$, and\n$b(X)\\le b(S\\cup D)$ iff $|D|-|o(D)|\\le\\delta$ with\n$\\delta=|Y|+1-\\open(q,S)$, where $o(D)$ is the union of the $o(d,S)$. By the\ndeficiency form of Hall's theorem the largest matching has size\n$|Y|-\\max_D(|D|-|o(D)|)$, which is at least $|Y|-\\delta=\\open(q,S)-1$ exactly\nwhen the repair holds.\n\\end{proof}\n\nThe bipartite graph has at most $\\close-1$ customers on one side and $\\open$\nstacks on the other, and the matching can stop at $\\open-1$ edges. Every $q$\nwith $\\open(q,S)\\le1$ passes at once, so the published rule is right\nthere~\\leanref{open1}.\n\n\\subsection{The other rules}\n\\label{sec:other-rules}\n\nEach of the remaining rules is quoted, proved sound in the form used, and\nshown to need the test or tie-break it carries.\n\n\\paragraph{The subset rule.} ``If $o(c_i,S)\\subseteq o(c_j,S)$ and $i<j$ \\dots\\\nmove $j$ can be removed'' \\citep[\\S2, p.~5]{ChuStuckey2009}. As implemented,\n$d$ dominates $r$ when $o(d,S)\\subseteq o(r,S)$ and the inclusion is strict or\n$d<r$. Every dominated playable $r$ is dropped, with a fallback that keeps $P$\nif all would go. The covering is sound for every set $S$: if\n$o(d,S)\\subseteq o(r,S)$ and $r$ is playable, then $d$ is playable and\n$\\Sol_k(S\\cdot r)\\Rightarrow \\Sol_k(S\\cdot d)$, because $d$ is free after $r$\nand closing $d$ then $r$ reaches the same state at no greater cost. With the\nindex tie-break, domination is a strict order, so an undominated move with a\nsolution survives, and the fallback never fires when a solution\nexists~\\leanref{subset}. The tie-break is necessary: without it, two customers\nwith equal new stacks dominate each other and both go, which loses a solution\non a 7-customer graph~\\leanref{tiebreak}.\n\n\\paragraph{The better move.} \\citet[\\S3.2, p.~6]{ChuStuckey2009}: ``Suppose\nS ++ [q] and S ++ [r, q] are playable and close(q, S $\\cup$ \\{r\\}) $\\ge$\nopen(q, S $\\cup$ \\{r\\}) then if U$'$ = S ++ [r] ++ R is a solution there exists a\nsolution U = S ++ [q] ++ R$'$.'' Its premise is the definite move's premise for\n$q$ at the child $\\cl(S\\cup\\{r\\})$, together with playability of $r$ then $q$,\nand its conclusion swaps $q$ and $r$~\\leanref{isbetter}. The swap is sound,\nbecause both orders end at the same state and neither step overflows. The\nappeal to the definite move is not.\n\n\\emph{Counterexample.} On the graph of Counterexample~\\ref{cex:definite} at the\nroot with $k=6$, the definite move does not fire, customers 0 and 2 both\nsurvive the subset rule, and the premise holds for $r=2$, $q=0$, so the rule\ndrops 2 citing 0. But $\\{2\\}$ has a solution and $\\{0\\}$ has\nnone~\\leanref{bettercex}. At that node another kept customer still has a\nsolution, so this is a false link and not a lost node; no node where the\nbetter move loses the last solution has been found. \\citet[Thm~6.3.8,\np.~143]{Chu2011} states the better move with a different premise,\n$\\close(q,S)\\ge\\open(q,S\\cup\\{r\\})$, and proves it through his Theorem~6.3.6.\nThe same graph refutes it at $S=\\{2\\}$, $r=3$, $q=0$~\\leanref{thm638}.\n\n\\emph{Repair and test.} Cite $q$ for $r$ only if $q$ is hereditarily definite\nat $\\cl(S\\cup\\{r\\})$, which is one matching at the child\n(Theorem~\\ref{thm:matching}); then the swap argument goes\nthrough~\\leanref{repbetter}.\n\n\\emph{Two requirements.} The close count in the premise must not include the\ncustomers that $r$ finishes on its own; counting them makes the rule's own\nconclusion false on a 12-customer graph. And the rules must run in the order\ndefinite, subset, better, each citing only customers still standing: if the\nbetter move runs first, the subset rule can cite a customer the better move\nhas already dropped, and an 8-customer graph loses a node through a cycle of\nlinks that are each true~\\leanref{bugs}. The second is an instance of the\ngeneral observation of \\citet[Prop.~3, Example~6]{Beck2025} that mutually\ndominating transitions must be tie-broken.\n\n\\paragraph{The old move.} \\citet[Thm~3, p.~7]{ChuStuckey2009} let a child\ninherit a sibling $q$ whose subtree failed, provided that inserting $q$ before\neach later step keeps that step playable (line~9 of\nAlgorithm~\\ref{alg:search}). The rule is sound as implemented: if\n$\\cost(S\\cup\\{q\\},c)\\le k$, then\n$\\Sol_k(\\cl(\\{q\\}\\cup S\\cdot c))\\Rightarrow\\Sol_k(S\\cdot q)$, so a refuted\nsibling stays refuted along any path whose steps pass the test~\\leanref{oldmove}.\nThe test is necessary; the smallest graph that shows it is the path on five\nvertices~\\leanref{reinsert}.\n\n\\paragraph{The memo.} Whether a state with $b(S)\\le k$ has a solution depends\non the state alone, not on the path that reached it~\\leanref{memo}. One\nconcern is that the memo and the old move might not combine: a refutation\nobtained with inherited old moves seems to depend on the ancestors, and one\nimplementation of the search declines to run both for that reason. A node\ntaken on its own, with an old move that is not in fact refuted, can indeed\nrecord a solvable state~\\leanref{fakeold}. Theorem~\\ref{thm:runs}\n(Appendix~\\ref{app:machinery}) shows that this never happens in a real run,\nbecause every old move is a refutation completed earlier in the same run.\n\n\\subsection{The repaired search is sound}\n\\label{sec:theorem}\n\n\\paragraph{What a rule must satisfy.} At a state $S$ with old moves $Q$, the\ncost cut keeps the playable candidates $P$ and the filter keeps a sublist\n$L\\subseteq P$. The filter is \\emph{node-sound} at $(S,Q)$ if, whenever every\n$q\\in Q$ has $\\neg\\Sol_k(S\\cdot q)$, $\\Sol_k(S)$ implies $\\Sol_k(S\\cdot c)$ for\nsome $c\\in L$~\\leanref{nodesound}. In terms of coverings, a node is sound when\ntwo things hold. First, every link a rule cites is a true covering. Second,\nevery chain of links from a discarded candidate ends in $L$ or in $Q$. The\nsecond condition can fail through a cycle even when every link is true, which\nis why the order of the rules matters. Proposition~\\ref{prop:filter} shows that\nthe repaired filter is node-sound, and Theorem~\\ref{thm:runs} that node\nsoundness, the memo and the old move together give sound runs; both are in\nAppendix~\\ref{app:machinery}. Together they give the main theorem.\n\n\\begin{theorem}[the revised search is sound]\\label{thm:main}\nRun the customer search with free moves, the memo, old moves and the filter\ndefinite $\\to$ subset $\\to$ better, the definite move firing only on a\nhereditarily definite customer and the better move citing only under the\nrepaired premise. If it refutes $k$ from the root, then no closing order costs\nat most $k$, $\\pw(G)>k-1$, and on the MOSP graph of an instance $M$ with at\nleast one 1, $Z(M)>k$~\\leanref{main}.\n\\end{theorem}\n\nIn words: each rule throws away only moves that a kept move is proved to be at\nleast as good as, and the memo and the old moves remember only failures that\nreally happened earlier in the same search. So when the search says ``no'',\nno closing order fits in $k$ stacks.\n\n\\paragraph{What is proved and what is tested.} The theorem is about the search\nas specified; that our C and Python implementations run it is checked, not\nproved. Three checks link them:\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item a transcription of the Lean filter agreed with the implemented filter on\n  16,244,090 node checks over graphs on 1 to 17 vertices;\n\\item the matching agreed with the hereditary premise on 36,939,226 candidates;\n\\item a search written from the theorems agreed with both implementations and\n  an exact oracle on 17,431,232 runs over 52,592 graphs on 1 to 17 vertices.\n\\end{itemize}\nSection~\\ref{sec:certs} adds a certificate checker for individual\nrefutations.\n\n\\paragraph{The published rules.} Under the published rules the filter is not\nnode-sound~\\leanref{notsound}. In a check of 570,206 runs with memo and old\nmove, it lost the last solution at a node in 58 runs, all on graphs built\naround Counterexample~\\ref{cex:definite} and all at $k$ equal to the optimum.\nEvery one found a solution through another branch and answered correctly. No\nwhole instance on which the published rules answer \\emph{unsat} wrongly is\nknown.\n\n\\subsection{The rules in pathwidth language}\n\\label{sec:layouts}\n\nThe definite move has an exact counterpart in the pathwidth literature, and\nthis subsection makes the correspondence precise.\n\\citet[p.~142]{Kitsunai2016}, stating Tamaki's notion, call an extension $\\tau$\nof a vertex sequence $\\sigma$ \\emph{committable} when\n$|\\partial X'|\\ge|\\partial V(\\tau)|$ for every $X'$ with\n$V(\\sigma)\\subseteq X'\\subseteq V(\\tau)$. Their Lemma~1 says that if $\\sigma$\nextends to a feasible permutation, so does $\\tau$.\n\n\\begin{lemma}\\label{lem:endpoint}\nFor $q\\notin S$ and $X=\\cl(S\\cup\\{q\\})$: the published premise holds iff\n$b(X)\\le b(S)$, the commitment condition at the single set $X'=S$; and the\nrepaired premise holds iff $X$ is a commitment from $S$, the condition at every\n$X'$ between them~\\leanref{endpoint}.\n\\end{lemma}\n\nSo Theorem~\\ref{thm:repaired} is the commitment lemma at the child, and the\npublished definite move is a commitment whose interior condition was dropped.\nIn Counterexample~\\ref{cex:definite} the endpoint test passes with\n$b(X)=3=b(S)$, while the interior set $\\{2,3,4\\}$ has border\n2~\\leanref{cexnotcommit}. The published premise is not wasted: by\n\\citet[Lemma~10, Cor.~2, pp.~148--149]{Kitsunai2016} it guarantees a\ncommitment to the least-border set between $S$ and $X$, which need not contain\n$q$~\\leanref{existscommit}. In the counterexample that set is\n$\\{2,3,4\\}$~\\leanref{commit234}. Committing there when the matching fails is a\nsecond repair that gives up no pruning; we have not built it.\n\nA commitment of depth one, $S$ to $S\\cup\\{q\\}$, holds iff\n$\\open(q,S)\\le1$~\\leanref{depth1}. The greedy step of\n\\citet[Lemma~3, p.~49]{CoudertMazauricNisse2014} is exactly that\ncondition~\\leanref{greedy}, and the push rule of\n\\citet[Rule~1, p.~330]{SuchanVillanger2009} is a case of it. So every published\npathwidth rule of this kind lies in the region $\\open\\le1$, where the published\ndefinite move is right; \\citet[p.~388, Table~2]{KobayashiKomuroTamaki2014}\nreport depth-one commitments as ``extremely effective''. Chu and Stuckey's\ndefinite move reaches into $\\open\\ge2$, and that is where it fails. For the\ncost of the test, \\citet[Cor.~2]{Kitsunai2016} find the least border between\nthe ends of a given extension by a minimum $s$--$t$ separator;\nTheorem~\\ref{thm:matching} is that computation for the single target $X$, where\nit becomes a bipartite matching.\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:known} sums up, and its rows are grouped by what they say. The\npublished definite and better moves are false. The free move, the memo and\nthe repaired definite move are known pathwidth results. The subset rule, the repaired\nbetter move and the old move have no counterpart we found, and are proved\nhere, as is the matching test. The comparison is limited to the pathwidth\npapers we hold\n\\citep{SuchanVillanger2009,Bodlaender2012,CoudertMazauricNisse2014,CoudertMazauricNisse2016,KobayashiKomuroTamaki2014,Kitsunai2016};\n``none found'' is not a claim of priority.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\\small\n\\caption{Two published rules are false; the free move, the memo and the\nrepaired definite move are known pathwidth results; the subset rule, the\nrepaired better move and the old move, like the matching test, are proved here.\nEach rule of the customer search against the exact-pathwidth literature.}\n\\label{tab:known}\n\\begin{tabularx}{\\linewidth}{@{}>{\\raggedright}p{3.2cm}>{\\raggedright}X>{\\raggedright\\arraybackslash}p{4.1cm}@{}}\n\\toprule\nRule & Published counterpart & Status \\\\\n\\midrule\ndefinite move, published & -- & false (Cex.~\\ref{cex:definite}) \\\\\nbetter move, published & none found & false \\\\\n\\midrule\nfree move & full sets \\citep{SuchanVillanger2009}; \\citet[Prop.~3]{Kitsunai2016} & known \\\\\ndefinite move, $\\open\\le1$ & depth-one commitments; greedy step; push rule & known \\\\\ndefinite move, repaired & commitment lemma \\citep[Lemma~1]{Kitsunai2016} & known; matching test (Thm~\\ref{thm:matching}) not found \\\\\nmemo & subset DP \\citep{Bodlaender2012}; prefix and failure tables & known; with the old move, proved here (Thm~\\ref{thm:runs}) \\\\\n\\midrule\nsubset rule & none found & proved here \\\\\nbetter move, repaired & none found & proved here \\\\\nold move & none found & proved here \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabularx}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Prior reports.} We found no earlier report of the error in the 56\nworks we read in full of the 141 that cite the customer search's papers\n(Appendix~\\ref{app:prior}). The pathwidth literature has the correct form of\nthe rule, as a commitment, and none of the pathwidth papers we hold mentions\nMOSP.\n","numLines":562,"startLine":1,"totalLines":562}}}
