{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TeMXC8Y3vZHSXiWoRCyxcX","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t% Section 5, computational results and the dataset (loop0009 item 06; was old\n2\t% Sections 4.6-4.7). Sources: paper2/solver_fix.md (cost, re-certification, the\n3\t% split and its addendum), paper2/certificates.md (proof object),\n4\t% paper2/dataset.md (the dataset, its checker and its price). Tables from\n5\t% tables/ (make_tables.py). Setup: lscpu and free on the machine that ran\n6\t% everything, read 2026-10-06; compiler flags from satisfiability/native.py.\n7\t\\section{Computational results and a dataset}\n8\t\\label{sec:results}\n9\t\n10\tSection~\\ref{sec:search} proved the repaired search sound. This section asks\n11\tfour practical questions about it and answers each with a measurement. What\n12\tdoes the repair cost? Under the repaired rules, the total number of search\n13\tnodes over each benchmark set changes by at most 2\\%. Do the published\n14\tbenchmark optima survive? All 115 that rested on the published rules were\n15\tproved again, and none changed. Can a reader check a refutation without\n16\ttrusting our code? Yes, up to about 75 customers, through a certificate and an\n17\tindependent checker. Finally, what does it make possible? A dataset of 17,714\n18\tgraphs from twenty benchmark collections. For 16,087 of them the pathwidth is\n19\tcertified, and that gives the exact value of every problem in the exact core.\n20\t\n21\t\\subsection{Experimental setup}\n22\t\\label{sec:setup}\n23\t\n24\t\\paragraph{Machine and code.} Every run in this section used one\n25\tmachine, an AMD Ryzen~9 9950X (16 cores, 32 hardware threads) with 128\\,GB of\n26\tmemory under Ubuntu Linux. Both searches, the open-stacks customer search and\n27\tits port to graphs, are written in C (gcc 15.2, \\texttt{-O3\n28\t-march=native}) and driven from Python~3.12. A Python implementation of the\n29\tsame rules serves as a reference for cross-checks and emits the certificates\n30\tof Section~\\ref{sec:certs}. The repaired tests are the default in every\n31\timplementation; the published tests remain available as an option.\n32\t\n33\t\\paragraph{Runs.} The basic job asks one question: is the optimum of this\n34\tinstance at most $k$? A \\emph{worker} is one single-threaded process that\n35\tanswers such questions, one at a time. A \\emph{node} is one state visited by\n36\tthe search, the unit in which we report effort. Up to 32 workers ran at\n37\tonce on the machine, so wall-clock times carry load noise. We therefore\n38\tcompare node counts and report time only in core-hours. A \\emph{pair} runs\n39\tthe same question twice in the same worker, once under the published rules\n40\tand once under the repaired rules, alternating which goes first. A\n41\t\\emph{descent} solves a whole graph: it lowers $k$ from an upper bound until a\n42\trefutation proves the last value optimal. Each table states its time or node\n43\tbudget.\n44\t\n45\t\\paragraph{Configurations.} The open-stacks runs use two configurations of the\n46\trules of Section~\\ref{sec:search}, which differ only in the better move. The\n47\t\\emph{base} configuration leaves it off. The configuration \\emph{with better\n48\tmove} switches it on for instances with at most five products per customer on\n49\taverage. This is the setting under which the customer search certified the\n50\tbenchmark values it proved.\n51\t\n52\t\\paragraph{Instances.} The \\emph{MOSP corpus} is the 6,376 instance files of\n53\tfive public open-stacks collections: the 2005 Constraint Modelling Challenge\n54\t\\citep{SmithGent2005} with a second copy of 46 of its files, the random\n55\tinstances of \\citet{ChuStuckey2009} (30 to 125 customers), those of\n56\t\\citet{FaggioliBentivoglio1998}, and the 24 industrial SCOOP instances. They\n57\tare the first rows of Table~\\ref{tab:dataset}. A Chu--Stuckey name such as\n58\t\\texttt{Random-125-125-2-4\\_0} gives the number of customers, the number of\n59\tproducts, the generator's density class and the instance number. The graph\n60\tbenchmarks are those of Section~\\ref{sec:names} and Table~\\ref{tab:dataset}.\n61\t\n62\t\\paragraph{Provenance.} Every stored value comes with a witness, an order that\n63\tattains it, and one of three provenances:\n64\t\\begin{itemize}\n65\t\\item \\emph{certified by search}: a search proved that no order does better,\n66\t  by refuting the value minus one;\n67\t\\item \\emph{certified by bound}: a lower bound equals the witness's value, so\n68\t  no search was needed;\n69\t\\item \\emph{upper bound only}: the witness is all there is, and optimality is\n70\t  not proved.\n71\t\\end{itemize}\n72\tOf the 6,376 corpus values, 6,374 are certified and two are upper bounds only.\n73\t\n74\t\\subsection{The cost of the repair, and the benchmark values}\n75\t\\label{sec:practice}\n76\t\n77\tThe repaired search adds one matching test to the definite move and to the\n78\tbetter move (Section~\\ref{sec:definite}). A candidate that fails the matching\n79\tis passed over, so the repaired tree can only differ where the matching fails.\n80\tTable~\\ref{tab:cost} shows how rarely that matters.\n81\t\n82\t\\begin{table}[t]\n83\t\\centering\\small\n84\t\\caption{The repair never changed an answer and changes the total node count\n85\tby at most 2\\%. Each pair runs the same question under the published and the\n86\trepaired rules in one worker. Totals are over the pairs that finished under\n87\tboth rules; a pair stopped by its budget says nothing about cost.\n88\t\\emph{Largest pair ratio}: the largest ratio of repaired to published nodes\n89\tover single pairs. Open-stacks pairs refute the optimum minus one, at 600\\,s per\n90\tcall at 50--100 customers and $2\\times10^8$ nodes at 125. The graph pairs run\n91\twhole descents on VSPLIB, the colouring and named graphs, and a sample of 500\n92\tRome graphs.}\n93\t\\label{tab:cost}\n94\t\\resizebox{\\linewidth}{!}{\\input{tables/cost}}\n95\t\\end{table}\n96\t\n97\tIn every row of Table~\\ref{tab:cost} the repaired and the published rules gave\n98\tthe same answer, and the total node counts differ by at most 2\\%. Single pairs\n99\tdiffer by at most 4\\%. The cost per node is also small: about 2\\% in the C\n100\timplementation, measured on six hard instances at 75 to 125 customers. The\n101\tpublished test passes and the matching then fails on only 0.07--0.27\\% of\n102\tdefinite-move candidates, a rate that rises with size.\n103\t\n104\t\\paragraph{The benchmark values survive.} No published optimum changed under\n105\tthe repaired rules. Of the 6,374 certified values in the MOSP corpus, 6,259 do\n106\tnot depend on the customer search at all. They were proved by an exhaustive\n107\tcomputation over all subsets of customers (up to 15 customers), by a SAT\n108\trefutation whose proof an independent checker accepted (in the DRAT format,\n109\tthe standard proof format for SAT solvers), by an earlier SAT search, or by a\n110\tlower bound equal to the value. The other 115 rested on the customer search\n111\tunder the published rules. The repaired search refuted the value minus one on\n112\tall 115, so all 115 values stand (as of 6 October 2026).\n113\t\n114\tThe seven hardest are 125-customer Chu--Stuckey instances at density classes 2\n115\tand 4. They are too large for one worker, so their search was split:\n116\tthe top levels of the tree are expanded into independent subtrees, each\n117\trefuted on its own worker, and the value is refuted when every subtree is. The\n118\tsplit is proved sound~\\leanref{split}. All seven were refuted at 18 to 369\n119\tcore-hours each, 1,112.6 in total (Table~\\ref{tab:split},\n120\tAppendix~\\ref{app:split}).\n121\t\n122\t\\paragraph{Two values in flight.} The corpus also holds two values that were\n123\tnever certified by any method: 25 for \\texttt{Random-125-125-2-2\\_0} and 21 for\n124\t\\texttt{Random-125-125-2-3\\_0}. The same split is refuting the value minus one\n125\ton each. As of 6 October 2026 it has refuted 2,141 and 2,354 subtrees, using\n126\t431 and 434 core-hours, and found no order of the smaller value. If the run\n127\tfinishes it certifies both values; an order of the smaller value would\n128\timprove the stored value instead.\n129\t\n130\t\\subsection{A checkable proof of a refutation}\n131\t\\label{sec:certs}\n132\t\n133\tA refutation by the search is a claim about a tree of up to $10^{12}$ nodes,\n134\tand Theorem~\\ref{thm:main} proves the rules sound, not the code that runs\n135\tthem. So the search emits a \\emph{certificate}: the tree it explored, every\n136\tpruning step with its rule and its witness, and for each definite and better\n137\tstep the matching of Theorem~\\ref{thm:matching}. An independent checker, which\n138\tshares no code with the search, reads the instance matrix and the certificate.\n139\tAt every node it checks that\n140\t\\begin{itemize}\n141\t\\item the premise of every pruning step holds, recomputed from the matrix;\n142\t\\item every matching is valid: distinct customers, distinct stacks, each stack\n143\t  newly opened by its customer, and at least $\\open-1$ edges;\n144\t\\item every candidate within the bound is either a child or covered by a\n145\t  pruning step, and every chain of covers ends at a child or an old move,\n146\t  with no cycle;\n147\t\\end{itemize}\n148\tand it replays the tree from the root. Each accepted step is a Lean theorem.\n149\tThat an acyclic covering by such steps is sound is a short paper argument, not\n150\tyet a formal proof.\n151\t\n152\t\\begin{table}[t]\n153\t\\centering\\small\n154\t\\caption{Every certificate the search emitted verifies, none is rejected, and\n155\tchecking takes milliseconds. Certificates for the repaired rules, configuration\n156\twith better move, refuting the optimum minus one on the certified MOSP corpus\n157\tat 9--75 customers, with 60\\,s to emit each. \\emph{Not emitted}: the emitter\n158\tdid not finish in time. Size is the compressed certificate; check time is the\n159\tindependent checker's.}\n160\t\\label{tab:certs}\n161\t\\input{tables/certificates}\n162\t\\end{table}\n163\t\n164\tTable~\\ref{tab:certs} gives the results. Every refutation emitted within its\n165\tbudget verifies, 6,275 of 6,286, and none is rejected. All 35.5\\,MB of\n166\tcertificates check in 147\\,s. The base configuration gives the same picture:\n167\t6,276 of 6,286 verify and none is rejected. The checker also catches the error\n168\tof Section~\\ref{sec:definite}. On the graph of\n169\tCounterexample~\\ref{cex:definite}, rooted at $\\{2\\}$, the published rules emit\n170\ta complete tree that claims there is no solution. A checker of the published\n171\tpremises accepts it. Ours rejects it, even with a largest matching attached,\n172\tbecause the matching has one edge where two are needed. At 125 customers the\n173\ttrees are too large to ship as certificates.\n174\t\n175\t\\subsection{A dataset with certified pathwidth}\n176\t\\label{sec:dataset}\n177\t\n178\tSection~\\ref{sec:equiv} showed that one certified pathwidth $w$ answers every\n179\tproblem in the exact core: pathwidth, vertex separation and the vertex\n180\tseparator game take the value $w$, and MOSP, gate matrix layout with multiple\n181\tfolding, one-dimensional logic, interval thickness, narrowness and node search\n182\ttake $w+1$. We therefore built one dataset from every benchmark collection of\n183\tthose problems that we could obtain, including the MOSP corpus.\n184\t\n185\tThe unit of the dataset is the \\emph{isomorphism class}: the set of instances\n186\twhose graphs are the same up to renaming the vertices. For a matrix instance\n187\tthe graph is its MOSP graph. Two instances in one class have the same value\n188\tunder every problem, so the dataset holds one record per class, with its\n189\tmembers and the renaming that maps each onto the record's graph. Classes were\n190\tfound with nauty, a standard tool that computes a canonical form for each\n191\tgraph. Each record carries the graph, the width $w$, its provenance, and a\n192\twitness layout of exactly that width. A record certified by bound also carries\n193\ta \\emph{minor certificate}: a list of vertex deletions and edge contractions\n194\tafter which every vertex has degree at least $w$. Any checker can replay it,\n195\tand it proves the pathwidth is at least $w$.\n196\t\n197\t\\begin{table}[t]\n198\t\\centering\\small\n199\t\\caption{Twenty collections, 21,754 instance files, reduce to 17,714\n200\tisomorphism classes, and 16,087 of them have a certified pathwidth. The open\n201\tclasses are mostly in the PACE sets, the VSPLIB grids and Harwell-Boeing\n202\tgraphs, and the MOSP sets of Carvalho and Soma and of Frinhani et al. \\emph{Classes}: isomorphism classes within the\n203\tcollection (78 graphs above 5,000 vertices are compared by file content only).\n204\t\\emph{Counted here}: classes whose first member, in the order of the table,\n205\tis in this collection, so the column sums to the dataset's class count. The\n206\tnext four columns split those classes by provenance; \\emph{No witnessed\n207\tvalue}: no layout is stored yet. $^{a}$The same 46 files distributed a second\n208\ttime under other names; every class is counted under its first copy.}\n209\t\\label{tab:dataset}\n210\t\\resizebox{\\linewidth}{!}{\\input{tables/dataset}}\n211\t\\end{table}\n212\t\n213\tTable~\\ref{tab:dataset} gives the counts. The collections are the MOSP\n214\tcollections\n215\t\\citep{SmithGent2005,FaggioliBentivoglio1998,ChuStuckey2009,CarvalhoSoma2015,Frinhani2018},\n216\televen VLSI gate matrix circuits \\citep{OliveiraLorena2002}, the VSPLIB sets\n217\t\\citep{Duarte2012} and the Small graphs \\citep{MartiCamposPinana2008}, the Rome\n218\tgraphs \\citep{DiBattista1997}, and treewidth collections used as pathwidth\n219\tbenchmarks \\citep{TreewidthLIB,freetdi,PACE2016,PACE2017}. Of the 17,714\n220\tclasses, 16,087 carry a certified pathwidth with a witness layout and 862 a\n221\twitnessed upper bound. The 610 large instances of \\citet{Frinhani2018}, with\n222\t400 to 1,000 customers, are included so that the dataset covers every\n223\tpublished MOSP collection we hold; none of them has a value yet, because an\n224\texact search at that size is out of reach.\n225\t\n226\tAn independent checker, sharing no code with the solvers, verifies the\n227\tdataset. It re-reads all 20,987 member files with its own readers, maps each\n228\tonto its record's graph by its renaming, recomputes the width of every witness\n229\tlayout and replays every minor certificate. It reports no failure on the\n230\t16,949 records. A value certified by search cannot be checked from its record;\n231\tit rests on the repaired search, and for instances within reach of the emitter\n232\tthe certificate of Section~\\ref{sec:certs} is how a third party can check it.\n233\t\n234\t\\paragraph{Agreement with published values.} All 84 Small graphs agree with\n235\tthe pathwidths of \\citet[Tables~4--5]{Mallach2018}. Ten of the eleven VLSI\n236\tcircuits are certified at their published best-known track counts\n237\t\\citep[Table~I]{OliveiraLorena2002}; the eleventh, W4 with 202 nets, is open\n238\tat 28 against 27.\n239\t\n240\t\\paragraph{A caution about MOSP files.} A MOSP file can be read with rows as\n241\tcustomers or with rows as patterns, and the two readings give different\n242\tgraphs. The same public repository ships the\n243\t\\citet{CarvalhoSoma2015} instances as customers by patterns and the\n244\t\\citet{ChuStuckey2009} instances beside them as patterns by customers. Only\n245\tpublished densities and values tell them apart. Read the wrong way round, an\n246\tinstance certifies at a different value.\n247\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b19c00ce-b033-4bb1-a352-ad2cf0b0fbf8","uuid":"133a2295-cb06-44b5-bfad-dffc99cbb90b","timestamp":"2026-10-06T18:44:03.978Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/al/dev/MOSP/paper2/latex/sec5_results.tex","content":"% Section 5, computational results and the dataset (loop0009 item 06; was old\n% Sections 4.6-4.7). Sources: paper2/solver_fix.md (cost, re-certification, the\n% split and its addendum), paper2/certificates.md (proof object),\n% paper2/dataset.md (the dataset, its checker and its price). Tables from\n% tables/ (make_tables.py). Setup: lscpu and free on the machine that ran\n% everything, read 2026-10-06; compiler flags from satisfiability/native.py.\n\\section{Computational results and a dataset}\n\\label{sec:results}\n\nSection~\\ref{sec:search} proved the repaired search sound. This section asks\nfour practical questions about it and answers each with a measurement. What\ndoes the repair cost? Under the repaired rules, the total number of search\nnodes over each benchmark set changes by at most 2\\%. Do the published\nbenchmark optima survive? All 115 that rested on the published rules were\nproved again, and none changed. Can a reader check a refutation without\ntrusting our code? Yes, up to about 75 customers, through a certificate and an\nindependent checker. Finally, what does it make possible? A dataset of 17,714\ngraphs from twenty benchmark collections. For 16,087 of them the pathwidth is\ncertified, and that gives the exact value of every problem in the exact core.\n\n\\subsection{Experimental setup}\n\\label{sec:setup}\n\n\\paragraph{Machine and code.} Every run in this section used one\nmachine, an AMD Ryzen~9 9950X (16 cores, 32 hardware threads) with 128\\,GB of\nmemory under Ubuntu Linux. Both searches, the open-stacks customer search and\nits port to graphs, are written in C (gcc 15.2, \\texttt{-O3\n-march=native}) and driven from Python~3.12. A Python implementation of the\nsame rules serves as a reference for cross-checks and emits the certificates\nof Section~\\ref{sec:certs}. The repaired tests are the default in every\nimplementation; the published tests remain available as an option.\n\n\\paragraph{Runs.} The basic job asks one question: is the optimum of this\ninstance at most $k$? A \\emph{worker} is one single-threaded process that\nanswers such questions, one at a time. A \\emph{node} is one state visited by\nthe search, the unit in which we report effort. Up to 32 workers ran at\nonce on the machine, so wall-clock times carry load noise. We therefore\ncompare node counts and report time only in core-hours. A \\emph{pair} runs\nthe same question twice in the same worker, once under the published rules\nand once under the repaired rules, alternating which goes first. A\n\\emph{descent} solves a whole graph: it lowers $k$ from an upper bound until a\nrefutation proves the last value optimal. Each table states its time or node\nbudget.\n\n\\paragraph{Configurations.} The open-stacks runs use two configurations of the\nrules of Section~\\ref{sec:search}, which differ only in the better move. The\n\\emph{base} configuration leaves it off. The configuration \\emph{with better\nmove} switches it on for instances with at most five products per customer on\naverage. This is the setting under which the customer search certified the\nbenchmark values it proved.\n\n\\paragraph{Instances.} The \\emph{MOSP corpus} is the 6,376 instance files of\nfive public open-stacks collections: the 2005 Constraint Modelling Challenge\n\\citep{SmithGent2005} with a second copy of 46 of its files, the random\ninstances of \\citet{ChuStuckey2009} (30 to 125 customers), those of\n\\citet{FaggioliBentivoglio1998}, and the 24 industrial SCOOP instances. They\nare the first rows of Table~\\ref{tab:dataset}. A Chu--Stuckey name such as\n\\texttt{Random-125-125-2-4\\_0} gives the number of customers, the number of\nproducts, the generator's density class and the instance number. The graph\nbenchmarks are those of Section~\\ref{sec:names} and Table~\\ref{tab:dataset}.\n\n\\paragraph{Provenance.} Every stored value comes with a witness, an order that\nattains it, and one of three provenances:\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\emph{certified by search}: a search proved that no order does better,\n  by refuting the value minus one;\n\\item \\emph{certified by bound}: a lower bound equals the witness's value, so\n  no search was needed;\n\\item \\emph{upper bound only}: the witness is all there is, and optimality is\n  not proved.\n\\end{itemize}\nOf the 6,376 corpus values, 6,374 are certified and two are upper bounds only.\n\n\\subsection{The cost of the repair, and the benchmark values}\n\\label{sec:practice}\n\nThe repaired search adds one matching test to the definite move and to the\nbetter move (Section~\\ref{sec:definite}). A candidate that fails the matching\nis passed over, so the repaired tree can only differ where the matching fails.\nTable~\\ref{tab:cost} shows how rarely that matters.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\\small\n\\caption{The repair never changed an answer and changes the total node count\nby at most 2\\%. Each pair runs the same question under the published and the\nrepaired rules in one worker. Totals are over the pairs that finished under\nboth rules; a pair stopped by its budget says nothing about cost.\n\\emph{Largest pair ratio}: the largest ratio of repaired to published nodes\nover single pairs. Open-stacks pairs refute the optimum minus one, at 600\\,s per\ncall at 50--100 customers and $2\\times10^8$ nodes at 125. The graph pairs run\nwhole descents on VSPLIB, the colouring and named graphs, and a sample of 500\nRome graphs.}\n\\label{tab:cost}\n\\resizebox{\\linewidth}{!}{\\input{tables/cost}}\n\\end{table}\n\nIn every row of Table~\\ref{tab:cost} the repaired and the published rules gave\nthe same answer, and the total node counts differ by at most 2\\%. Single pairs\ndiffer by at most 4\\%. The cost per node is also small: about 2\\% in the C\nimplementation, measured on six hard instances at 75 to 125 customers. The\npublished test passes and the matching then fails on only 0.07--0.27\\% of\ndefinite-move candidates, a rate that rises with size.\n\n\\paragraph{The benchmark values survive.} No published optimum changed under\nthe repaired rules. Of the 6,374 certified values in the MOSP corpus, 6,259 do\nnot depend on the customer search at all. They were proved by an exhaustive\ncomputation over all subsets of customers (up to 15 customers), by a SAT\nrefutation whose proof an independent checker accepted (in the DRAT format,\nthe standard proof format for SAT solvers), by an earlier SAT search, or by a\nlower bound equal to the value. The other 115 rested on the customer search\nunder the published rules. The repaired search refuted the value minus one on\nall 115, so all 115 values stand (as of 6 October 2026).\n\nThe seven hardest are 125-customer Chu--Stuckey instances at density classes 2\nand 4. They are too large for one worker, so their search was split:\nthe top levels of the tree are expanded into independent subtrees, each\nrefuted on its own worker, and the value is refuted when every subtree is. The\nsplit is proved sound~\\leanref{split}. All seven were refuted at 18 to 369\ncore-hours each, 1,112.6 in total (Table~\\ref{tab:split},\nAppendix~\\ref{app:split}).\n\n\\paragraph{Two values in flight.} The corpus also holds two values that were\nnever certified by any method: 25 for \\texttt{Random-125-125-2-2\\_0} and 21 for\n\\texttt{Random-125-125-2-3\\_0}. The same split is refuting the value minus one\non each. As of 6 October 2026 it has refuted 2,141 and 2,354 subtrees, using\n431 and 434 core-hours, and found no order of the smaller value. If the run\nfinishes it certifies both values; an order of the smaller value would\nimprove the stored value instead.\n\n\\subsection{A checkable proof of a refutation}\n\\label{sec:certs}\n\nA refutation by the search is a claim about a tree of up to $10^{12}$ nodes,\nand Theorem~\\ref{thm:main} proves the rules sound, not the code that runs\nthem. So the search emits a \\emph{certificate}: the tree it explored, every\npruning step with its rule and its witness, and for each definite and better\nstep the matching of Theorem~\\ref{thm:matching}. An independent checker, which\nshares no code with the search, reads the instance matrix and the certificate.\nAt every node it checks that\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item the premise of every pruning step holds, recomputed from the matrix;\n\\item every matching is valid: distinct customers, distinct stacks, each stack\n  newly opened by its customer, and at least $\\open-1$ edges;\n\\item every candidate within the bound is either a child or covered by a\n  pruning step, and every chain of covers ends at a child or an old move,\n  with no cycle;\n\\end{itemize}\nand it replays the tree from the root. Each accepted step is a Lean theorem.\nThat an acyclic covering by such steps is sound is a short paper argument, not\nyet a formal proof.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\\small\n\\caption{Every certificate the search emitted verifies, none is rejected, and\nchecking takes milliseconds. Certificates for the repaired rules, configuration\nwith better move, refuting the optimum minus one on the certified MOSP corpus\nat 9--75 customers, with 60\\,s to emit each. \\emph{Not emitted}: the emitter\ndid not finish in time. Size is the compressed certificate; check time is the\nindependent checker's.}\n\\label{tab:certs}\n\\input{tables/certificates}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:certs} gives the results. Every refutation emitted within its\nbudget verifies, 6,275 of 6,286, and none is rejected. All 35.5\\,MB of\ncertificates check in 147\\,s. The base configuration gives the same picture:\n6,276 of 6,286 verify and none is rejected. The checker also catches the error\nof Section~\\ref{sec:definite}. On the graph of\nCounterexample~\\ref{cex:definite}, rooted at $\\{2\\}$, the published rules emit\na complete tree that claims there is no solution. A checker of the published\npremises accepts it. Ours rejects it, even with a largest matching attached,\nbecause the matching has one edge where two are needed. At 125 customers the\ntrees are too large to ship as certificates.\n\n\\subsection{A dataset with certified pathwidth}\n\\label{sec:dataset}\n\nSection~\\ref{sec:equiv} showed that one certified pathwidth $w$ answers every\nproblem in the exact core: pathwidth, vertex separation and the vertex\nseparator game take the value $w$, and MOSP, gate matrix layout with multiple\nfolding, one-dimensional logic, interval thickness, narrowness and node search\ntake $w+1$. We therefore built one dataset from every benchmark collection of\nthose problems that we could obtain, including the MOSP corpus.\n\nThe unit of the dataset is the \\emph{isomorphism class}: the set of instances\nwhose graphs are the same up to renaming the vertices. For a matrix instance\nthe graph is its MOSP graph. Two instances in one class have the same value\nunder every problem, so the dataset holds one record per class, with its\nmembers and the renaming that maps each onto the record's graph. Classes were\nfound with nauty, a standard tool that computes a canonical form for each\ngraph. Each record carries the graph, the width $w$, its provenance, and a\nwitness layout of exactly that width. A record certified by bound also carries\na \\emph{minor certificate}: a list of vertex deletions and edge contractions\nafter which every vertex has degree at least $w$. Any checker can replay it,\nand it proves the pathwidth is at least $w$.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\\small\n\\caption{Twenty collections, 21,754 instance files, reduce to 17,714\nisomorphism classes, and 16,087 of them have a certified pathwidth. The open\nclasses are mostly in the PACE sets, the VSPLIB grids and Harwell-Boeing\ngraphs, and the MOSP sets of Carvalho and Soma and of Frinhani et al. \\emph{Classes}: isomorphism classes within the\ncollection (78 graphs above 5,000 vertices are compared by file content only).\n\\emph{Counted here}: classes whose first member, in the order of the table,\nis in this collection, so the column sums to the dataset's class count. The\nnext four columns split those classes by provenance; \\emph{No witnessed\nvalue}: no layout is stored yet. $^{a}$The same 46 files distributed a second\ntime under other names; every class is counted under its first copy.}\n\\label{tab:dataset}\n\\resizebox{\\linewidth}{!}{\\input{tables/dataset}}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:dataset} gives the counts. The collections are the MOSP\ncollections\n\\citep{SmithGent2005,FaggioliBentivoglio1998,ChuStuckey2009,CarvalhoSoma2015,Frinhani2018},\neleven VLSI gate matrix circuits \\citep{OliveiraLorena2002}, the VSPLIB sets\n\\citep{Duarte2012} and the Small graphs \\citep{MartiCamposPinana2008}, the Rome\ngraphs \\citep{DiBattista1997}, and treewidth collections used as pathwidth\nbenchmarks \\citep{TreewidthLIB,freetdi,PACE2016,PACE2017}. Of the 17,714\nclasses, 16,087 carry a certified pathwidth with a witness layout and 862 a\nwitnessed upper bound. The 610 large instances of \\citet{Frinhani2018}, with\n400 to 1,000 customers, are included so that the dataset covers every\npublished MOSP collection we hold; none of them has a value yet, because an\nexact search at that size is out of reach.\n\nAn independent checker, sharing no code with the solvers, verifies the\ndataset. It re-reads all 20,987 member files with its own readers, maps each\nonto its record's graph by its renaming, recomputes the width of every witness\nlayout and replays every minor certificate. It reports no failure on the\n16,949 records. A value certified by search cannot be checked from its record;\nit rests on the repaired search, and for instances within reach of the emitter\nthe certificate of Section~\\ref{sec:certs} is how a third party can check it.\n\n\\paragraph{Agreement with published values.} All 84 Small graphs agree with\nthe pathwidths of \\citet[Tables~4--5]{Mallach2018}. Ten of the eleven VLSI\ncircuits are certified at their published best-known track counts\n\\citep[Table~I]{OliveiraLorena2002}; the eleventh, W4 with 202 nets, is open\nat 28 against 27.\n\n\\paragraph{A caution about MOSP files.} A MOSP file can be read with rows as\ncustomers or with rows as patterns, and the two readings give different\ngraphs. The same public repository ships the\n\\citet{CarvalhoSoma2015} instances as customers by patterns and the\n\\citet{ChuStuckey2009} instances beside them as patterns by customers. Only\npublished densities and values tell them apart. Read the wrong way round, an\ninstance certifies at a different value.\n","numLines":247,"startLine":1,"totalLines":247}}}
