{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WGChAg4r1wKQS4rjpfX62u","type":"tool_result","content":"Notation. Table 2.1 lists the symbols used in the rest of the paper. Graphs are finite and simple. A 0/1\nmatrix M has the customers as rows and the patterns as columns; in the VLSI problems the rows are nets\nand the columns gates. Row r is active at position j of a column order π if it has 1s in two columns x, y with\nπ(x) ≤ j ≤ π(y), possibly x = y: its stack is open there. “M has a 1” excludes only the all-zero matrix. One\nletter needs care. In this section k is the bound in each decision problem, so for pathwidth it bounds a width.\nIn Section 4 k bounds the number of open stacks, which is one more than a width; we say so again there.\n\n2.1   The problems\nEach problem is a decision problem with an instance and an integer k; its value is the least k for which the\nanswer is yes. Four of the twelve carry the rest of the paper, and we define them in full. Figure 1.1 shows\neach on one instance.\nPathwidth pw(G). Is there a sequence of bags X1 , . . . , Xr ⊆ V covering V , with every edge inside some bag,\n     Xi ∩ Xℓ ⊆ X j whenever i ≤ j ≤ ℓ, and |Xi | ≤ k + 1? (Kinnersley, 1992, p. 346). The condition on\n     Xi ∩ Xℓ is the interval property: the bags that contain a vertex are consecutive.\nVertex separation vs(G). Is there a layout L such that for every i < n at most k vertices u with L(u) ≤ i\n     have a neighbour v with L(v) > i? (Kinnersley, 1992, p. 346).\nMOSP Z(M). Given a 0/1 matrix M of customers by patterns, is there an order of the patterns with at most\n   k customers active at every position? (Linhares and Yanasse, 2002, eqs. (1)–(2)).\nGate matrix layout t(M). Given a net–gate matrix, is there an order of the gates and an assignment of nets\n     to k tracks such that nets active at a common position get different tracks? (Möhring, 1990, p. 18);\n     (Wing et al., 1985, Problem 1).\n   The other eight problems are needed only to read the table. Table 2.2 gives each in one line; Ap-\npendix B.1 gives the formal definitions, with the sources.\n\n                                                      4\n\fTable 2.1: Notation. Each symbol has one meaning throughout the paper; the symbols of the search are defined in\nSection 4.1.\n\nSymbol                     Meaning\nG = (V, E), n = |V |          a graph, its vertices and edges; in Section 4 the vertices are the customers, V = C\nL                             a layout, a bijection V → {1, . . . , n}\nX1 , . . . , Xr               the bags of a path decomposition\nM; C; P                       a 0/1 matrix; its rows (customers, nets); its columns (patterns, gates)\nGM                            the MOSP graph: vertex set C, two customers adjacent when some pattern has a 1 in both\n                              rows; each pattern becomes a clique\nH                             the net graph of a circuit: two nets adjacent when they share a gate\nπ                             an order of the columns\nZ(M), t(M)                    the MOSP value (fewest open stacks) and the gate matrix layout value (fewest tracks)\npw, vs                        pathwidth and vertex separation\nθ , ns, mns, es, pes, ν, sb, cw,\n                              themcw,\n                                  values\n                                      VSG,of the\n                                              pla other problems (Table 2.2)\nk                             the bound of a decision problem; in Section 4, a bound on open stacks, so width k − 1\nw                             a width, as in “pathwidth w”\nN[c]                          the closed neighbourhood of c: c and its neighbours\n∂ T , b(T )                   the border of a vertex set T , the vertices outside T with a neighbour in T ; b(T ) = |∂ T |\n\n\n\n\nTable 2.2: The other eight problems of the Linhares–Yanasse table look unrelated (two circuit layouts, two search\ngames and four graph problems), yet each asks for the least k that is possible. One line each. Formal definitions are in\nAppendix B.1.\n\nProblem                       In one line\nOne-dimensional logic         Order the gates of a circuit so that the intervals spanned by the nets overlap at most k at\n                              any point. Its graph is the net graph H, which joins two nets that share a gate. A\n                              boundary variant pins two gates to the ends.\nPLA folding pla(M)            Gate matrix layout in at most k tracks, with at most two nets on a track. With no limit per","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b19c00ce-b033-4bb1-a352-ad2cf0b0fbf8","uuid":"3491a302-15cf-4472-ba79-47b63bcdead7","timestamp":"2026-10-06T18:47:09.868Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"Notation. Table 2.1 lists the symbols used in the rest of the paper. Graphs are finite and simple. A 0/1\nmatrix M has the customers as rows and the patterns as columns; in the VLSI problems the rows are nets\nand the columns gates. Row r is active at position j of a column order π if it has 1s in two columns x, y with\nπ(x) ≤ j ≤ π(y), possibly x = y: its stack is open there. “M has a 1” excludes only the all-zero matrix. One\nletter needs care. In this section k is the bound in each decision problem, so for pathwidth it bounds a width.\nIn Section 4 k bounds the number of open stacks, which is one more than a width; we say so again there.\n\n2.1   The problems\nEach problem is a decision problem with an instance and an integer k; its value is the least k for which the\nanswer is yes. Four of the twelve carry the rest of the paper, and we define them in full. Figure 1.1 shows\neach on one instance.\nPathwidth pw(G). Is there a sequence of bags X1 , . . . , Xr ⊆ V covering V , with every edge inside some bag,\n     Xi ∩ Xℓ ⊆ X j whenever i ≤ j ≤ ℓ, and |Xi | ≤ k + 1? (Kinnersley, 1992, p. 346). The condition on\n     Xi ∩ Xℓ is the interval property: the bags that contain